WEBVTT

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:13.750
<v Ira Glass>There's this story my mom used to like to tell. My older sister, Randi, was eight or so when this happened. And something came up at school that Randi didn't understand, and she came home. And she asked my mom, where do babies come from?
<v Ira Glass>我妈以前挺喜欢讲这样一个故事。这事发生时，我大姐兰迪（Randi）大概八岁左右。当时学校里遇到了一个兰迪不太明白的问题，她就回家了。她问我妈妈，小宝宝是从哪里来的？

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:38.880
<v Ira Glass>And my mom just felt like, great. This is great. She's bringing this up on her own. I don't have to sit her down in some awkward birds and bees conversation someday. We can get this out of the way now when she's young, and she's really excited to tell her this stuff. And she explains the whole thing, right? Where babies come from, what sex is. And the way my mom always used to tell the story, she said that she felt like, I am doing such a good job here. Like, I am such a good mom today.
<v Ira Glass>我妈当时的感觉就是，太好了。这太棒了。她居然自己主动提起这事。总算不用以后找一天尴尬地跟她坐下来谈什么“蜜蜂和花朵”的故事了。趁她还小，现在就能把这事给解决了，而且我妈其实挺高兴能跟她说这些的。于是她把整套理论全解释了一遍，对吧？小宝宝是从哪来的，什么是性。按我妈一贯的叙事风格，她说当时觉得自己，我这事办得太棒了。就觉得，我今天简直是个绝世好妈妈。

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:59.810
<v Ira Glass>And at the end of it, she asked Randi, OK, so that's everything. Do you have any questions? And then Randi pauses for a long moment, and she asks, "Can birds really fly?" And my mom realizes, of course, oh, she was not ready. Like, she absorbed nothing.
<v Ira Glass>在讲完最后，她问兰迪，好了，事情就是这样。你有什么问题吗？然后兰迪沉默了很久，最后问了一句：“鸟儿真的会飞吗？”我妈这才意识到，好吧，她根本没准备好。也就是说，她什么都没听进去。

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:26.000
<v Ira Glass>And, sure enough, like, a year passes, and Randi comes back to her-- I don't remember why-- and asked her again, like, where do babies come from? And it's like the first time never happened. She remembered none of it. And I think of that story sometimes when there are debates over what is suitable for children. And I think there's a whole category of things that kids protect themselves from. Like, they just don't want to engage with that stuff. Adults don't need to keep them from it.
<v Ira Glass>果不其然，大约过了一年，兰迪又来找她——我记不清是因为什么了——又问了一遍，小宝宝是从哪来的？这就好像头一次压根没发生过一样。她把之前听的全给忘了。每当看到关于什么内容才适合儿童的争论时，我有时就会想起这个故事。我觉得有一大类事情，孩子们是有自我保护意识的。比如说，他们根本不想去接触那些东西。成年人其实没必要刻意把他们隔离开。

00:01:26.000 --> 00:02:08.940
<v Ira Glass>But then there's other stuff, stuff that does seep into their world that they take to heart and they mull about and they make part of themselves. And the thing that's difficult is that it can be really hard to predict what those things are going to be. Well, today, on our program, things that kids hear, things they see, things that happen to them that affect them in ways that they only understand later as adults. Like, for instance, what it means to give little Black kids toys that celebrate the Confederacy, or to drag teenage boys into the desert to watch atomic bombs go off, or to bring your kid to work when your work involves breaking the law. How do you even quantify exactly what that does to a person?
<v Ira Glass>但还有另一些东西，那些确实渗入他们世界的东西，他们会往心里去，会反复琢磨，并将其变成自我的一部分。难点在于，你很难预测究竟哪些东西会产生这样的影响。那么今天，在我们的节目里，我们要聊聊孩子们听到的话、看到的事、经历过的遭遇，这些是如何在日后他们成年时才真正理解的方式来影响他们的。比如，给黑人小孩送庆祝邦联的玩具意味着什么，把十几岁的男孩拉到沙漠里去看原子弹爆炸是怎么回事，或者带自己的孩子去上班而你的工作恰好是违法的又作何感想。你究竟该如何量化这会对一个人造成什么样的影响呢？

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:24.680
<v Ira Glass>Well, today, we try to figure that out. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天，我们就来探究个究竟。这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台的《这美利坚生活》（This American Life）。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。请继续收听。

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:28.580
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Stars and Bars and Bars.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕：星条旗与牢笼。

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:38.880
<v Ira Glass>We got the idea for this week's show from this thing that happened to one of our producers, Neil Drumming, a realization that he came to an adulthood about a TV show that he'd watched as a kid. Here's Neil.
<v Ira Glass>本期节目的创意源于我们的一位制作人尼尔·德拉明（Neil Drumming）身上发生的一件事，那是他在成年后对小时候看过的一部电视剧产生的一种领悟。下面有请尼尔。

00:02:38.880 --> 00:03:11.440
<v Neil Drumming>The hit TV show, "The Dukes of Hazzard," ran from 1979 to 1985. It starred John Schneider and Tom Wopat as Bo and Luke Duke, two rakish, good ol' boys trying to stay on the right side of the law in rural Georgia. But if you ask any man my age who was a fan of the show, "The Dukes of Hazzard's" true star was a bright orange 1969 Dodge Charger nicknamed the General Lee. Every week on CBS, the General Lee would roar through country backroads, leaping over virtually everything, defying the laws of physics, and blowing little boys' minds across America.
<v Neil Drumming>热播电视剧《正义前锋》（The Dukes of Hazzard）于1979年至1985年播出。主演约翰·施奈德（John Schneider）和汤姆·沃帕特（Tom Wopat）饰演博（Bo）和卢克（Luke）·杜克兄弟俩，这是一对玩世不恭的好小伙，在佐治亚州的乡村里努力遵纪守法。但如果你问任何一个我这个年纪、曾是该剧粉丝的男人，《正义前锋》真正的明星其实是一辆亮橙色的1969年道奇战马（Dodge Charger），绰号叫“李将军”（General Lee）。每周在CBS电视台，这辆“李将军”都会咆哮着穿过乡间小路，飞跃几乎所有的障碍，无视物理定律，震撼着全美无数小男孩的心灵。

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:12.700
I had a General Lee.
我曾有过一辆“李将军”。

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.500
<v Neil Drumming>In what, like, a Matchbox or a Hot Wheels?
<v Neil Drumming>是那种，火柴盒小车（Matchbox）还是风火轮（Hot Wheels）？

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:17.210
No, I had a straight up, like, the plastic joint.
不，我有一辆货真价实的，就是那种塑料玩具车。

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:18.390
<v Neil Drumming>Oh. OK, like a big sized one.
<v Neil Drumming>哦。好吧，就是那种大号的。

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:18.970
The big sized one.
大号的那种。

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:19.470
<v Neil Drumming>OK.
<v Neil Drumming>好的。

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:25.680
You know, whatever, go around. Drive it over your dad's foot, and he'd get angry. Like that size.
你知道的，随便玩。从你爸脚上开过去，然后他就会生气的。就那种尺寸。

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:44.350
<v Neil Drumming>This is one of my favorite rappers, Breeze Brewin. He's from the Bronx. Like me, he watched "The Dukes of Hazzard" faithfully as a boy. We both had "Dukes of Hazzard" lunch boxes, and we were both proud owners of miniature versions of the General Lee. Back then, it was probably one of the most coveted toys any kid in either of our neighborhoods could possess.
<v Neil Drumming>这位是我最喜欢的说唱歌手之一，布里斯·布鲁因（Breeze Brewin）。他来自布朗克斯。和我一样，他小时候也是《正义前锋》的忠实观众。我们俩都有《正义前锋》的饭盒，而且我们都骄傲地拥有“李将军”的模型。在当年，这可能是我们两个社区里任何一个孩子都梦寐以求的玩具之一。

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:47.680
<v Neil Drumming>I remember the bully of the neighborhood stole mine and tried to pretend it was his.
<v Neil Drumming>我记得社区里有个恶霸偷了我的，然后还假装那是他的。

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:53.620
I hate when that happens. This is mine, right? I'm like, no, it's definitely not.
我最恨这种事了。这是我的，对吧？我就说，不，这绝对不是。

00:03:53.620 --> 00:04:16.980
<v Neil Drumming>The General Lee was remarkable. Not just because it was both fast and furious before that was a thing, but because its rooftop was emblazoned with the Confederate flag. As kids, Black kids, pushing our mini American muscle along the carpets and concrete of New York City, neither Breeze nor I saw anything bothersome about that bold symbol up top. In fact, for us at the time, the rebel flag was just part of what made the car so damn cool.
<v Neil Drumming>“李将军”非常了不起。不仅仅因为它在那个概念诞生之前就兼具了速度与激情，还因为它车顶上印有醒目的邦联旗帜。作为小孩子，作为推着我们的迷你美国肌肉车在地毯和纽约市混凝土路面上玩耍的黑小孩，无论是布里斯还是我，都没有觉得车顶上那个醒目的标志有什么不妥。事实上，对那时的我们来说，叛军旗帜只是让这辆车如此酷炫的一部分原因而已。

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:33.800
<v Breeze Brewin>There was a kid in my neighborhood, Irish kid. He had an orange Dodge Challenger 440, no flag on the top. I remember a lot of us were like, damn, he need to throw the flag on top of that bad boy.
<v Breeze Brewin>我社区里有个小孩，是个爱尔兰小孩。他有一辆橙色的道奇挑战者440，车顶上没有旗帜。我记得我们很多人都觉得：靠，他真得在那个酷车顶上搞一面旗帜才行。

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:35.140
<v Neil Drumming>Wait, it was a real car, or it was a toy?
<v Neil Drumming>等等，那是真车，还是玩具？

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:39.500
<v Breeze Brewin>No, he drove that bad boy, and you heard him coming down the block.
<v Breeze Brewin>不，他开的是真车，你听见声音就知道他开进街区了。

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:40.790
<v Neil Drumming>You're just like, that car would be dope.
<v Neil Drumming>你当时只会觉得，那车太帅了。

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:49.320
<v Breeze Brewin>That car would be dope if he had that flag on that bad boy.
<v Breeze Brewin>如果那辆酷车顶上有那面旗帜，那就更帅了。

00:04:49.320 --> 00:05:09.320
<v Neil Drumming>I got the idea to talk to Breeze about this because, in 2001, as part of his group, the Juggaknots, he wrote and released a song called "Generally." It's spelled like the adverb, but a play on the name of the Dukes' prized racer. I don't remember exactly when I heard the song, but by then, it had been over 15 years since "The Dukes of Hazzard" went off the air. I hadn't thought about the show or the car in ages.
<v Neil Drumming>我想跟布里斯聊聊这个，是因为在2001年，作为他的组合Juggaknots的一部分，他写并发行了一首歌，名叫《Generally》。它的拼写虽然像副词，但其实是对杜克兄弟那辆珍贵赛车的名字的一语双关。我不记得自己具体是什么时候听到这首歌的，但到了那时，《正义前锋》停播已经超过15年了。我已经很久没有想起过这部电视剧或那辆车了。

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:10.060
<v Neil Drumming>[MUSIC PLAYING - "GENERALLY" BY JUGGAKNOTS]
<v Neil Drumming>[音乐播放——JUGGAKNOTS乐队的《GENERALLY》]

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:20.360
<v Juggaknots>That was my favorite, y'all. General Lee. They was them good ol' boys. General Lee. They wasn't meaning no harm. General Lee. You watched the show. You had the toys.
<v Juggaknots>那是我的最爱，各位。李将军。他们就是那帮好小伙。李将军。他们可没安什么坏心眼。李将军。你看过这节目。你玩过这玩具。

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:43.080
<v Neil Drumming>I'm pretty sure that when I was in my 20s and heard "Generally," well, that was the first time I put two and two together and realized, oh, wait. One of my favorite toys growing up actually bore an unabashed affront to my humanity and freedom. The flag on the roof-- how the hell did I miss that? Listening to the song again recently, I thought maybe it was about Breeze himself coming to that same realization. We are the same age after all.
<v Neil Drumming>我很确信，当我二十几岁听到《Generally》这首歌时，那是我第一次把两件事联系到一起，并意识到：哦，等等。我从小最喜欢的玩具之一，竟然公然冒犯了我和平等的权利与自由。车顶上的旗帜——我当时到底是怎么忽略掉的呢？最近再次听到这首歌时，我想也许这首歌讲述的就是布里斯自己得出同样认识的过程。毕竟我们是同龄人。

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:57.030
<v Neil Drumming>But when he and I met to talk about it, Breeze said he'd recognized the inherent put down in the General Lee's paint job by the time he was in high school. He was actually inspired to write "Generally" a few years later, after he'd had kids of his own. One day, he took them on a shopping trip.
<v Neil Drumming>但当我和他见面聊起此事时，布里斯说，早在上高中时，他就已经意识到“李将军”车身涂装中蕴含的贬低意味了。事实上，几年后在有了自己的孩子之后，他才受到启发创作了《Generally》。有一天，他带着孩子们去购物。

00:05:57.030 --> 00:06:14.580
<v Breeze Brewin>I was literally in Toys "R" Us, probably on Gun Hill Road near Co-op City, or the one in Yonkers on Central Ave, and kids losing their shh, looking for toys, and me questioning, like, what's up with these toys?
<v Breeze Brewin>我当时真的在玩具反斗城（Toys "R" Us）里，可能是在库普城（Co-op City）附近的冈山路（Gun Hill Road）那家，或者是中央大道（Central Ave）扬克斯的那家，孩子们在里面疯狂地找玩具，而我在心里犯嘀咕，这些玩具到底是怎么回事？

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:15.080
<v Neil Drumming>Oh, OK.
<v Neil Drumming>哦，原来如此。

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:17.250
<v Breeze Brewin>And then I see the General Lee.
<v Breeze Brewin>然后我就看到了“李将军”。

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:23.700
<v Neil Drumming>The iconic roadster was back on the shelves, being pushed on a new generation to promote some TV movie relaunch.
<v Neil Drumming>这辆标志性的跑车又回到了货架上，为了配合某种电视电影的重新上映，它被推销给新一代的孩子们。

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:24.800
<v Neil Drumming>Do you remember it?
<v Neil Drumming>你还记得吗？

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:25.980
<v Breeze Brewin>What, the song?
<v Breeze Brewin>什么，这首歌？

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:26.480
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah.
<v Neil Drumming>对。

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:27.090
<v Breeze Brewin>Yeah, definitely.
<v Breeze Brewin>记得，当然记得。

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:30.010
<v Neil Drumming>Could you take me through it?
<v Neil Drumming>你能给我讲讲吗？

00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:31.350
<v Breeze Brewin>You want bars?
<v Breeze Brewin>你想听押韵（bars）？

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:32.160
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah, man.
<v Neil Drumming>对啊，老兄。

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:40.590
<v Breeze Brewin>Oh, man, it's tough. I'm 43. (RAPPING) You drive me batty. Daddy's survivin' and be seeming slimmin', screaming women. Could the sound be more annoying?
<v Breeze Brewin>噢，天哪，这挺沉重的。我43岁了。（说唱）你让我抓狂。老爸在苦苦支撑，显得日渐消瘦，尖叫的妇女。这声音还能再烦人点吗？

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:55.760
<v Neil Drumming>The thing I love about Breeze is that he writes these dense, clever, impressionistic rhymes that sound like words tumbling down stairs. In this first verse, he's telling the story of that trip to Toys "R" Us, building the scene of a frustrated parent overwhelmed by toys and demanding children.
<v Neil Drumming>我喜欢布里斯的一点在于，他写出的那些紧凑、聪明、富有印象派色彩的押韵听起来就像词语顺着楼梯滚落下来一样。在第一段主歌里，他讲述了去玩具反斗城的那趟行程，构建了一个被玩具和无理取闹的孩子搞得焦头烂额、心力交瘁的家长的场景。

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:05.250
<v Breeze Brewin>(RAPPING) Baggin' all these whack toys, trying to lack noise, peace and quiet. Cease a riot constructively. Then what I see? The General Lee.
<v Breeze Brewin>（说唱）把这些烂玩具都装进袋子，试图制止噪音，换取和平与安静。建设性地平息一场骚乱。然后我看到了什么？李将军。

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:38.270
<v Breeze Brewin>And I said, you know what? I need to explore this idea, like why do they think that was OK? Why do they think that they could show this image and make us champion that image? Why is it OK to make our pain this pop icon? Why was it OK? I just wanted to attack it, that you would have the audacity to do that. I was just thinking, not with my kids.
<v Breeze Brewin>当时我说，你知道吗？我得深入探讨一下这个想法，比如为什么他们会觉得这没问题？为什么他们会觉得可以展示这种形象，还让我们去拥护这种形象？为什么可以把我们的痛苦变成这种流行符号？为什么可以这样？我只是想抨击这种行为，你们竟然敢这么做。我当时就在想，绝不能让这种事发生在我孩子身上。

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:40.940
<v Neil Drumming>So then you go-- so then there's the chorus after that, right?
<v Neil Drumming>所以接下来你就——接着后面就是副歌了，对吧？

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:54.310
<v Breeze Brewin>Yeah. So the chorus it's like, (RAPPING) the General Lee, representing for your clan. The General Lee, for real, somebody pulled a fast one. The General Lee, now, I'm starting to overstand. The General Lee wasn't the first, won't be the last one.
<v Breeze Brewin>对。副歌部分是这样的：（说唱）李将军，代表着你的宗族。李将军，说真的，有人耍了花招。李将军，现在，我开始彻悟。李将军不是第一个，也绝不会是最后一个。

00:07:54.310 --> 00:08:01.880
<v Neil Drumming>I love that line, "somebody pulled a fast one," just because it's nice. It's like, the car is fast, and it's like a really-- it's a really nice one.
<v Neil Drumming>我特别喜欢那句词，“有人耍了花招”，纯粹是因为这句写得真好。这就好比，这车速度快，而且它就像一个真的——这是一个非常绝妙的隐喻。

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:04.690
<v Breeze Brewin>I appreciate that.
<v Breeze Brewin>我很感激你这么说。

00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:06.200
<v Neil Drumming>It's very slick.
<v Neil Drumming>非常巧妙。

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:09.370
<v Breeze Brewin>I mean, it's pretty blatant. It's the hoodwinking.
<v Breeze Brewin>我的意思是，这太显而易见了。这就是一场骗局。

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:10.130
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah.
<v Neil Drumming>对。

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:38.820
<v Breeze Brewin>They got over, you know? They normalized it, for better or for worse. It's a huge screw you, you know? It's like, we're going to do this. You're not going to say nothing. We're going to put it on prime time TV. We're going to put it in Toys "R" Us. In fact, you're going to buy it. So it's like-- in the song, I say, "But sell my L. Sell my loss."
<v Breeze Brewin>他们蒙混过关了，你知道吗？他们把这事给日常化了、合理化了，不管是好是坏。这简直是个巨大的“去你的”，你知道吗？就像是在说：我们就这么干了。你们什么也别想说。我们要把它搬上黄金档电视。我们要把它摆进玩具反斗城。事实上，你们还会掏钱买它。所以这就好比——我在歌里唱道，“但出卖我的L（失败/Loser/Lee）。出卖我的损失。”

00:08:38.820 --> 00:09:06.240
<v Neil Drumming>Of course, at seven, eight, nine years old, it wasn't Breeze and I controlling the TV remote or spending our hard-earned cash. It was ultimately our parents who let us watch the show and bought us the toys. Considering that both my parents were born and raised in rural South Carolina and experienced chilling racism firsthand, you'd think they would have objected to the Dukes and their General Lee. But they didn't. Breeze's parents, who are from the Caribbean, they didn't either. He has a theory as to what our parents were thinking.
<v Neil Drumming>当然，在七八九岁的时候，掌控电视遥控器或花掉我们辛苦赚来的钱的人并不是我和布里斯。最终还是我们的父母让我们看了这个节目，并给我们买的玩具。考虑到我的父母都是在南卡罗来纳州乡村出生长大的，并且亲身经历过令人心寒的种族主义，你本以为他们会反对杜克兄弟和他们的“李将军”。但他们并没有。布里斯的父母来自加勒比海地区，他们也没有反对。对于我们父母当时的想法，他有一个理论。

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:18.450
<v Breeze Brewin>I think, you know, it's a lot about, I don't want my kids to be left out. I don't want my kids to be weird. It was a number one show. So it was like, you know, why deny them that?
<v Breeze Brewin>我觉得吧，很大程度上是因为，我不想让我的孩子被边缘化。我不想让我的孩子显得怪异。这是一档收视率第一的节目。所以大家就会觉得，为什么不让他们看呢？

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:33.390
<v Neil Drumming>OK, this is important. On TV, the doors on the General Lee were welded shut, as is common with a lot of stock race cars. So Bo and Luke Duke were constantly hopping through the windows. It was the only way in or out of the car. Here's Breeze with the song's final verse.
<v Neil Drumming>好的，这一点很关键。“李将军”车门在电视里是被焊死的，这在许多量产赛车中很常见。所以博和卢克·杜克经常要从车窗里跳进跳出。这是进出车辆的唯一方式。下面是这首歌的最后一段主歌。

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:39.160
<v Breeze Brewin>(RAPPING) Disturbable to see what we was murdered to when it coulda, shoulda been a convertible.
<v Breeze Brewin>（说唱）看到我们曾为何事惨遭谋杀，这真令人不安，本该、本可以把它改成敞篷车。

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:52.290
<v Breeze Brewin>I started off with like, you know what? You was constantly jumping in and out of the windows. It would have made more sense and been more practical to have been a convertible. Just cut the top off. Chop the top off.
<v Breeze Brewin>我一开始写的是，你知道吗？你们当时一直在车窗里跳进跳出。如果改成敞篷车，不是更合情合理、更实用吗？直接把车顶切掉。把车顶锯掉。

00:09:52.290 --> 00:10:03.030
<v Breeze Brewin>But you needed it. You needed the top of the car. You needed to show that image, because that's the pimpery. That's the game. I get it. Y'all wanted to see it. Here it is.
<v Breeze Brewin>但你们需要它。你们需要这个车顶。你们需要展示这个形象，因为这就是皮条客的把戏。这就是他们的套路。我懂的。你们就想看这个。那它就来了。

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:04.680
<v Neil Drumming>OK, take me through that verse.
<v Neil Drumming>好的，带我仔细品品这段主歌。

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:28.780
<v Breeze Brewin>(RAPPING) Disturbable to see what we was murdered to when it coulda, shoulda been a convertible. See that they're climbing through the windows and go make better time and do the pro hopping, but no stopping. Commercial use is the issue. They get you with decals. Match the lunchbox, pajamas. You wanna snatch the bunch. Lock that be wild, generally praise the stuff, crave the stuff, slaves to stuff. Get it for me, begging, like, please.
<v Breeze Brewin>（说唱）看到我们曾为何事惨遭谋杀，这真令人不安，本该、本可以把它改成敞篷车。看他们爬进爬出车窗，本可以节省时间，玩起专业跳车，却从不停歇。商业利用才是问题所在。他们用贴纸拿捏你。搭配饭盒、睡衣。你想把整套都抢光。疯狂锁定目标，对这东西顶礼膜拜，渴望这东西，沦为物质的奴隶。求着买给我，像这样苦苦哀求。

00:10:28.780 --> 00:10:42.000
<v Breeze Brewin>So now with that, I talked about how I would beg my parents for it. But then it's just, like, sporting the pain, you know what I mean? Like, you were sporting my pain, and it's literally a comedy.
<v Breeze Brewin>说到这儿，我就讲到了我是怎么向父母苦求这玩意的。但这其实就相当于——是在炫耀我的痛苦，你知道我的意思吗？就像是，你们当时在炫耀我的痛苦，而这居然活生生是一部喜剧。

00:10:42.000 --> 00:11:14.820
<v Breeze Brewin>(RAPPING) Sportin' the pain. Sure was a brainwashed kid, but never had a fad with rockin' a swastika. Of course, Nazi. But that I'd rather not see. The closest that I got be seek Haile Selassie. But sell my L, see it's nothing than this double standard. Trouble, be it something candid or this subtle. Gun the flag down. What? No hesitating. Gutter regulation, fuck the legislation. But now, some of y'all walking around like, for shame, word, as I wonder what the murderers of James Byrd thought about the General Lee.
<v Breeze Brewin>（说唱）炫耀痛苦。我确实曾是个被洗脑的孩子，但从来没对崇拜纳粹标志产生过什么狂热。当然，那是纳粹。但那种我宁愿不看。我能接近的最接近的信仰是寻觅海尔·塞拉西（Haile Selassie）。但出卖我的L，看哪，这不过是双重标准。麻烦，不管是坦诚相见还是这种潜移默化的暗涌。把旗帜轰下来！什么？没有犹豫。粗暴的规则，去他妈的立法。但现在，你们其中有些人走起路来好像在说：真可耻，讲真，我很好奇詹姆斯·伯德（James Byrd）的谋杀犯们对“李将军”是怎么看的。

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:32.360
<v Neil Drumming>In 1998, James Byrd, a Black man in Texas, was badly beaten and pissed on by three white supremacists. The men then chained Byrd by his ankles to a pickup truck and dragged him over asphalt for roughly three miles. Byrd died when his head and arm were severed from his body.
<v Neil Drumming>1998年，得克萨斯州的一名黑人男子詹姆斯·伯德遭到三名白人至上主义者的残酷殴打并被小便污辱。随后，这几名男子用铁链将伯德的脚踝拴在一辆皮卡后面，在柏油路上拖行了大约三英里。伯德的头部和手臂从躯体上被硬生生扯断，当场死亡。

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:48.090
<v Neil Drumming>The white supremacists, who sported various Nazi and confederate symbols, dumped Byrd's mutilated body near a Black church. This atrocity was heavy on Breeze's mind when he wrote "General Lee," which is why the last verse ends in anger. As he says, gun the flag down, no hesitating.
<v Neil Drumming>这些身饰各种纳粹和邦联标志的白人至上主义者，把伯德被残害的尸体丢弃在了一座黑人教堂附近。布里斯在写《General Lee》这首歌时，这起暴行沉重地压在他的心头，这也是为什么最后一段主歌以愤怒收尾。正如他所唱的，把旗帜轰下来，绝不迟疑。

00:11:48.090 --> 00:12:15.140
<v Breeze Brewin>I kind of was like, I know some people are going to be like, Breeze, you're wildin'. You're talking about shooting down a flag. You should go through the right channels. There's legislation. [LAUGHS] But I'm like, word? Well, what do the murderers of James Byrd think about the General Lee? You know what I mean? It's just like, at some point, we gotta kill your idols. We gotta burn your idols.
<v Breeze Brewin>我当时就觉得，我知道有些人会说：布里斯，你太疯狂了。你居然在谈论开枪打下一面旗帜。你应该通过正确的途径去解决。有相关立法呢。[笑]但我当时就想，真的吗？好吧，那詹姆斯·伯德的谋杀犯们是怎么看待“李将军”的呢？你懂我的意思吗？这就好像，在某个时刻，我们必须亲手毁灭你们的偶像。我们必须烧毁你们的偶像。

00:12:15.140 --> 00:12:19.660
<v Neil Drumming>So you were saying-- you told me that you were-- you've been thinking about this song a lot lately.
<v Neil Drumming>所以你刚才说——你告诉我，你——你最近一直在思考这首歌。

00:12:19.660 --> 00:12:37.690
<v Breeze Brewin>Literally, like a week before you called, I was listening to the song and I almost got a little choked up. Because like, god damn, you know what I mean? It's like, 16 years later, why is it still an issue? I'd like to think, come on, you're better than that now, America.
<v Breeze Brewin>真的，就在你打电话给我的前一个星期，我当时在听这首歌，听着听着我差点哽咽了。因为，靠，真见鬼，你知道我的意思吗？这就好比，16年过去了，为什么这依然是个问题？我本想认为，得了吧，美国，你们现在应该比这做得更好才对。

00:12:37.690 --> 00:12:43.830
<v Breeze Brewin>[LAUGHTER]
<v Breeze Brewin>[笑声]

00:12:43.830 --> 00:13:11.570
<v Neil Drumming>That said, I can't help but harbor a little bit of nostalgia for the General Lee. Not so much for the thing itself, but for what it represents for me-- a time before I could possibly recognize the insidiousness of its design, before I really understood its origins or how that history has shaped me and the people I care about, back when all I really had to worry about was who had the coolest toy on the block.
<v Neil Drumming>话虽如此，我还是忍不住对“李将军”怀有一丝淡淡的怀旧之情。与其说是对那个物品本身，不如说是为了它对我而言所代表的意义——那是一个我还无法识别其设计阴险之处的时期，一个在我真正理解它的历史渊源、或者这段历史如何塑造了我以及我所关心的人之前，那时我唯一需要担心的只是谁才是街区里拥有最酷玩具的人。

00:13:11.570 --> 00:13:17.910
<v Ira Glass>Neil Drumming. These days, he works at our sister company, Serial, making podcasts. Today's show is a rerun.
<v Ira Glass>尼尔·德拉明。如今他在我们的兄弟公司Serial工作，制作播客。今天的节目是重播。

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:27.350
<v Joralen Mauldin>I honestly believe they should have changed the name, Robert E. Lee High School, a very long time ago-- probably after, you know, '90s, early 2000s.
<v Joralen Mauldin>我真心认为他们早就该改名了，罗伯特·E·李高中（Robert E. Lee High School），很早以前就该改了——大概在，你知道的，90年代或者2000年代初的时候。

00:13:27.350 --> 00:13:44.490
<v Ira Glass>That's Joralen Mauldin. When we talked to her for today's program, she was 16 years old, a member of the drill team at Robert E. Lee High School in Tyler, Texas-- a Black girl with a blond afro, which, on Fridays, would be tucked under a white cowboy hat, part of her drill team uniform. She talked to B.A. Parker.
<v Ira Glass>那是约拉伦·莫尔丁。我们在今天的节目中采访她时，她16岁，是德克萨斯州泰勒市罗伯特·E·李高中的仪仗队成员——一个有着金色爆炸头的黑人女孩。每周五，这个爆炸头会塞进一顶白色的牛仔帽里，那是她仪仗队制服的一部分。她接受了B·A·帕克的采访。

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:59.460
<v Joralen Mauldin>I love being on this team. I love the girls who are on here. But you know, the history is still-- you know, it's there at the back of my mind. And I know a lot of it, it doesn't really bother that many people, but it's still something that I find that gets to me.
<v Joralen Mauldin>我喜欢加入这个团队。我喜欢这里的女孩们。但你知道的，那段历史依然——你知道的，它就在我脑海的深处。我知道很多历史其实并没有真正困扰那么多的人，但这依然是我发现会触动我的一点。

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:19.890
<v Ira Glass>That history is that Robert E. Lee High School was founded in 1958, four years after "Brown versus Board of Education," as a place for white students to go to avoid integration. The town gave it the name Robert E. Lee as a way to give the finger to the Civil Rights movement. The school prided itself on having this massive Confederate flag-- allegedly, the second-largest Confederate flag in the world.
<v Ira Glass>这段历史是，罗伯特·E·李高中建于1958年，即“布朗诉教育委员会案”四年之后，当时是为了给白人学生提供一个避开种族融合的地方。小镇给它取名罗伯特·E·李，以此来对民权运动竖起中指。学校曾为拥有这面巨大的邦联旗帜而感到骄傲——据称，这是世界上第二大的邦联旗帜。

00:14:19.890 --> 00:14:40.470
<v Ira Glass>It took 20 people to carry it. Then in the 1970s, the courts ordered the town to integrate the high school. These days, 60% of the school was Black and Latino. Though in Tyler, it is still referred to as the white high school. When they announce the name of the school at the beginning of games, Joralen says, she just kind of blocks it out. She found out about the school's history a little while ago.
<v Ira Glass>这需要20个人才能抬动。后来在20世纪70年代，法庭下令该镇将高中进行种族融合。如今，学校里60%是黑人和拉丁裔学生。尽管在泰勒市，它仍然被称为白人高中。约拉伦说，当他们在比赛开始时宣布学校的名字时，她只是选择将其屏蔽。不久前，她才了解到这所学校的历史。

00:14:40.470 --> 00:15:16.930
<v Joralen Mauldin>I learned this last year, I believe, because we have memory books. And it dates back all the way to when Lee first opened, and we were flipping through one of the very first ones. And that was when I saw the uniforms and all the traditions that they used to have, the Southern Belle Drill Team and our Bell Guards. They were called the Rebels and the Rebelettes, and they did wear Confederate uniform style of clothing and things like that, and they proudly displayed their Confederate flag. And it just shocked me, and all I could think about was, what? Like, for real? Y'all are kidding me right now.
<v Joralen Mauldin>我想我是去年了解到的，因为我们有纪念册。它一直追溯到李高中刚开学的时候，当时我们正在翻阅其中一本最早的纪念册。就在那时，我看到了制服以及他们过去拥有的所有传统，南方佳丽仪仗队和我们的铃铛卫队。他们被称为“叛军”和“叛军女孩”，他们确实穿着邦联制服风格的服装之类的内容，并且他们自豪地展示了他们的邦联旗帜。这让我感到震惊，我脑子里想的全部都是：什么？真的吗？你们现在是在跟我开玩笑吧。

00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:18.780
<v B.A. Parker>Do you have to sing the school song?
<v B.A. Parker>你们必须唱校歌吗？

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:22.560
<v Joralen Mauldin>They tried to make us learn it, but I never memorized it.
<v Joralen Mauldin>他们试图让我们学，但我从来没记住过。

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:32.510
<v Ira Glass>The school song starts with the lyrics, "Robert E. Lee, we raise our voices in praise of your name. May honor and glory guide you to fame."
<v Ira Glass>校歌的开头歌词是：“罗伯特·E·李，我们高声赞美你的名字。愿荣誉和光荣指引你走向辉煌。”

00:15:32.510 --> 00:15:34.770
<v B.A. Parker>Have you ever-- have ever looked at the lyrics of it?
<v B.A. Parker>你有没有——有没有看过滤面的歌词？

00:15:34.770 --> 00:15:38.720
<v Joralen Mauldin>I've looked at the lyrics, which is why I haven't tried to memorize it.
<v Joralen Mauldin>我看过歌词，这就是为什么我没有试图去记住它。

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:40.860
<v B.A. Parker>Would you ever sing it?
<v B.A. Parker>你这辈子会唱吗？

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:43.730
<v Joralen Mauldin>Never in my life, never.
<v Joralen Mauldin>我这辈子都不会，绝对不会。

00:15:43.730 --> 00:16:19.770
<v Ira Glass>There's one lyric in the school song-- "Our memories will bind us to Robert E. Lee." That part definitely seemed true back when we first aired this story in 2017. Then, in 2020, one year after Joralen graduated, the school district actually changed the name of the school to Tyler Legacy High School. Joralen, by the way, is not crazy about that name either. She says, "Legacy" is a nod to the school's Confederate legacy. She says she would have gone with something neutral-- like, for instance, Rose City High.
<v Ira Glass>校歌里有一句歌词——“我们的记忆将我们与罗伯特·E·李绑定在一起。”当我们2017年首次播出这个故事时，这部分听起来绝对是真实的。然后，在2020年，也就是约拉伦毕业一年后，学区实际上将学校名称改为了泰勒传承高中。顺便说一句，约拉伦对这个名字也不是很感冒。她说，“传承”是对学校邦联传统的暗示。她说她本会选择一个中立的名字——比如，罗斯城高中。

00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:20.270
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING - "WAITING FOR THE ROBERT E. LEE" BY DEAN MARTIN]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐播放 - 迪恩·马丁的《等待罗伯特·E·李》]

00:16:20.270 --> 00:16:34.430
<v Dean Martin>Way down on the levee in old Alabammy, there's daddy and mammy. There's Efraim and Sammy. On a moonlight night, you can find them all. While they're awaiting, the banjos are syncopating.
<v Dean Martin>沿着老阿拉巴马的堤防往下，有爸爸和妈妈。有埃弗雷姆和萨米。在一个月光皎洁的夜晚，你可以找到他们所有人。当他们等待时，班卓琴正在奏出切分音。

00:16:34.430 --> 00:16:47.840
<v Dean Martin>What's that they're sayin'? What's that they're sayin'? But while they keep playing, I'm humming and swayin'. It's the good ship "Robert E. Lee" that's come to carry the cotton away.
<v Dean Martin>他们在说什么？他们在说什么？但当他们继续演奏时，我在哼唱和摇摆。那是“罗伯特·E·李”号好船，它来把棉花运走了。

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:51.230
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, History is Not a Toy.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，历史不是玩具。

00:16:51.230 --> 00:17:10.170
<v Ira Glass>So there are over 200 public schools around the country named after Confederate leaders and over 700 monuments memorializing the confederacy in public places. Those numbers come from a database run by the Southern Poverty Law Center. Of course, many other statues have been taken down in the last few years. The Statue of Robert E. Lee was removed just this week in Richmond.
<v Ira Glass>所以全国有200多所公立学校以邦联领导人的名字命名，公共场所还有700多座纪念邦联的纪念碑。这些数字来自南方贫困法律中心运营的数据库。当然，过去几年里许多其他的雕像已经被拆除了。罗伯特·E·李的雕像就在本周在里士满被移除了。

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:23.780
<v Ira Glass>But for all this commemoration of white experience of life in the South before and during the Civil War, there's actually not that much out there memorializing the Black experience. One of the few places is in Baltimore. I have to say, I was surprised to learn it was there.
<v Ira Glass>但是，尽管有这么多对南方在内战前和内战期间白人生活经验的纪念，实际上并没有那么多纪念黑人经验的东西。少数几个地方之一在巴尔的摩。我必须说，得知它在那里我很惊讶。

00:17:23.780 --> 00:17:42.430
<v Ira Glass>I grew up in Baltimore, and OK, this came into existence after I moved away. But I'm back in Baltimore regularly to see family, though I had never heard of it, until B.A. Parker, who was a producer at our program-- and who also grew up in Baltimore, not far from where I grew up-- told me about it. Here she is.
<v Ira Glass>我在巴尔的摩长大，好吧，这是在我搬走之后才出现的。但我经常回巴尔的摩看望家人，尽管我从来没听说过它，直到我们节目的制作人B·A·帕克——她也在巴尔的摩长大，离我长大的地方不远——跟我提起它。她就在这里。

00:17:42.430 --> 00:18:01.120
<v B.A. Parker>Every Black kid in Baltimore knows this place. Most of us went there on field trips when we were in school or were taken there by an earnest parent. It's called the National Great Blacks in Wax Museum. That's great Blacks, and they're rendered in wax. It's the first Black History wax museum in the country.
<v B.A. Parker>巴尔的摩的每个黑人小孩都知道这个地方。我们大多数人在上学时都去那里进行过实地考察，或者被一个认真的父母带去过。它被称为国家伟人黑人蜡像馆。那是伟大的黑人，他们是用蜡制作的。它是全国第一座黑人历史蜡像馆。

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:19.980
<v B.A. Parker>I first went there on a field trip in the third grade and, for better or worse, it's been stuck in my memory ever since. To be honest, it scared the shit out of me. It's violent and graphic, and unlike any other museum I've ever been to. And its main clientele was children.
<v B.A. Parker>我第一次去那里是在三年级的一次实地考察中，无论好坏，从那以后它就一直印在我的记忆里。老实说，它把我吓死。它充满暴力且直白，与我去过的任何其他博物馆都不同。而它的主要客户群体是儿童。

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:44.960
<v B.A. Parker>Even today, I can talk to anybody who grew up Black and in Baltimore, and if you bring up the Blacks in Wax, they'll be like, yeah, that was messed up, right? Texting with friends and family about this last week, I got messages like, "I had nightmares" and "I started to tear up" and "I can still see it in my head." I wanted to go back to the museum to see if it was as bad as I remembered.
<v B.A. Parker>直到今天，我可以和任何在巴尔的摩长大的黑人交谈，如果你提起黑人蜡像馆，他们都会说，对，那太糟糕了，对吧？上周和朋友家人发短信时，我收到了诸如“我有噩梦”、“我开始流泪”以及“我脑子里还能看到它”之类的信息。我想回博物馆看看它是不是像我记忆中那么糟糕。

00:18:44.960 --> 00:18:48.540
<v B.A. Parker>There's a crowd of people from a bus coming in.
<v B.A. Parker>有一群坐大巴来的人。

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:50.420
<v Sean Cole>Oh, there's a couple kids.
<v Sean Cole>哦，有几个孩子。

00:18:50.420 --> 00:19:15.790
<v B.A. Parker>So I went back there recently with my colleague, Sean Cole. The museum itself is an old fire station and three row houses fused into one building. In the lobby, the wax figures of W.E.B. DuBois and Carter G. Woodson are there to greet you. Next to the ticket booth is a figure of the great military general Hannibal, riding a massive elephant. And up over ahead is the first Black female pilot, Bessie Coleman.
<v B.A. Parker>所以我最近和我的同事肖恩·科尔一起回到了那里。博物馆本身是一个旧消防站和三栋联排别墅融为一体的建筑。在大厅里，W·E·B·杜布依斯和卡特·G·伍德森的蜡像在那里迎接你。售票处旁边是伟大的军事统帅汉尼拔骑着一头庞大大象的蜡像。头顶上方是第一位黑人女性飞行员贝茜·科尔曼。

00:19:15.790 --> 00:19:18.580
<v Sean Cole>Oh, I didn't even see. There's an airplane above us.
<v Sean Cole>哦，我甚至没看见。我们头顶有一架飞机。

00:19:18.580 --> 00:19:25.690
<v B.A. Parker>There is an airplane above us with, like, a cool chick in it with her shades on, and she's taking no one's mess.
<v B.A. Parker>我们头顶上有一架飞机，里面有一个，比如，很酷的戴着墨镜的辣妹，她谁的账也不买。

00:19:25.690 --> 00:19:40.600
<v Ira Glass>The lobby's awe-inspiring and stuff, but then you buy your ticket and actually enter the museum. Then everything changes. A quick warning-- there's a lot of violence of all sorts in what follows. It might not be for everyone.
<v Ira Glass>大厅令人敬畏之类的，但接着你买票并真正进入博物馆。然后一切都变了。一个快速警告——接下来会有很多各种各样的暴力。它可能不适合所有人。

00:19:40.600 --> 00:20:01.360
<v Ira Glass>When you step inside the museum, it's dark. Immediately to the right, there's a wax figure of a slave. He's on his knees, his teeth knocked out, and he's being held down by two white slavers who pull his head back. They've shoved a long funnel into his mouth and are pouring gruel through it, force feeding him. Brown gruel spills out of his mouth.
<v Ira Glass>当你步入博物馆时，里面很暗。右边立刻有一个奴隶的蜡像。他跪在地上，牙齿被打掉，两个白人奴奴主把他按住，往后拉他的头。他们把一个长漏斗塞进他的嘴里，通过它倒入粗玉米粥，强行喂食他。褐色的粗玉米粥从他嘴里溢出来。

00:20:01.360 --> 00:20:14.660
<v Ira Glass>As an act of rebellion, some slaves wouldn't eat, so the slavers made them. This is the very first thing you see. And then to the left, is one of the museum's signature attractions.
<v Ira Glass>作为反抗的一种行为，一些奴隶不肯吃东西，所以奴奴主强迫他们吃。这是你看到的第一件事。然后左边是博物馆的标志性景点之一。

00:20:14.660 --> 00:20:18.200
<v B.A. Parker>All right, so do you want to go into the slave ship?
<v B.A. Parker>好吧，所以你想进奴隶船吗？

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:35.620
<v Ira Glass>They've built a facsimile of a slave ship into the museum. It's not that big, about the size of a large trailer. But when I was eight, it seemed enormous. Back then, walking down to the hold below was like walking onto an amusement park ride, only the opposite of fun.
<v Ira Glass>他们在博物馆里建造了一个奴隶船的复制品。它不是很大，大约有一个大拖车那么大。但当我八岁时，它看起来巨大无比。那时候，走到底层的船舱就像是走进游乐园的游乐设施，只是完全不是那么回事。

00:20:35.620 --> 00:21:12.920
<v Ira Glass>At the entrance, there are two white wax slave merchants, both impeccably dressed. One sits behind a desk. It used to be that when you stepped onto the stairs, a recording would play, one of the merchants announcing, "New load of slaves coming aboard now." At eight, I understood. They meant me. I was supposed to be a new slave. I burst into tears. At first, I refused to go all the way down into the ship. But the other kids did it, so I did it too. And what I saw when I got to the bottom of the steps was what I was seeing now, another vicious looking white man.
<v Ira Glass>在入口处，有两个白人奴隶商人，两人的穿着都无可挑剔。一个坐在桌子后面。过去，当你踏上楼梯时，会播放一段录音，其中一个商人的宣布：“新一批奴隶现在登船。”在八岁时，我明白了。他们指的是我。我应该成为一个新奴隶。我放声大哭。起初，我拒绝一直走到船舱底部。但其他孩子都去了，所以我也去了。当我走到台阶底部时，我看到的是我现在看到的另一个面目狰狞的白人男子。

00:21:12.920 --> 00:21:37.840
<v B.A. Parker>And he's branding a slave woman who has a chain around her neck and, like, placing the brand on her shoulder. And she's fully nude and covered in blood. Jeez, it's just-- pure agony is on her face. And he looks like he's full of hate and anger, and is just ready to torture someone. None of this has changed.
<v B.A. Parker>他正在给一个脖子上戴着链子的奴隶女性打烙印，比如，把烙印放在她的肩膀上。她全身赤裸，浑身是血。天哪，这简直是——她脸上有纯粹的痛苦。他看起来充满仇恨和愤怒，随时准备折磨某人。这一切都没有变。

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:42.500
<v Sean Cole>There's collections on shelves of just parts of bodies bloodied.
<v Sean Cole>架子上有仅仅是血淋淋的身体部分的收藏。

00:21:42.500 --> 00:21:50.840
<v B.A. Parker>Being eaten by rats. That should be noted. Like, there's one under-- like, below, that's dismembered and being eaten by rats.
<v B.A. Parker>被老鼠吃掉。这应该指出。比如，有一个在下面——比如，下面，被肢解并被老鼠吃掉。

00:21:50.840 --> 00:22:12.680
<v Ira Glass>The museum goes for an intense realism. It doesn't leave anything out. It's over the top in a way that's almost campy. This is probably because the wax figures all were made in the 1970s. The slavers have mutton chops. A few of the slaves look more like they're from "Good Times" than from "Roots." But the scenes are always based on documented atrocities.
<v Ira Glass>博物馆追求强烈的现实主义。它什么都不落下。它夸张到几乎有点俗气的程度。这可能是因为蜡像都是在20世纪70年代制作的。奴隶主留着羊排须。一些奴隶看起来更像是来自《美好时光》而不是《根》。但这些场景总是基于有据可查的暴行。

00:22:12.680 --> 00:22:36.770
<v Ira Glass>At a certain point in the slave ship, I turn around, and I see the entire wall behind me is one huge mirror. And then I see the chained-up slaves and I'm forced to look at myself. And it comes home to me in an instant that these people are my ancestors. Without the torture, without the sorrow, without the misery that they endured during the Middle Passage, there would be no me.
<v Ira Glass>在奴隶船上的某个时刻，我转过身，看到我身后的整面墙都是一面巨大的镜子。然后我看到了被锁起来的奴隶，我被迫看着自己。我瞬间明白了，这些人是我的祖先。没有中大西洋航程中忍受的折磨、没有悲伤、没有苦难，就不会有我。

00:22:36.770 --> 00:22:41.480
<v Sean Cole>So what do you remember from when you were a kid and seeing this?
<v Sean Cole>那么你还记得你小时候看到这个时的情景吗？

00:22:41.480 --> 00:23:03.410
<v B.A. Parker>I remember, like, being confused, right, and being scared. The fact that it's someone who's my skin, my-- you don't have the language for it. But it is, like, a pause and reflect, and your eyes grow wide. And you're trying to understand what it means.
<v B.A. Parker>我记得，比如，感到困惑，对吧，还有感到害怕。那是和我相同肤色的人，我——你没有语言来形容它。但它就像是一个停顿和反思，然后你瞪大了眼睛。你在试图理解这意味着什么。

00:23:03.410 --> 00:23:06.640
<v B.A. Parker>I'm sorry. I just got distracted by them trying to shove the guy in the oven. Sorry.
<v B.A. Parker>对不起。我刚才被他们试图把那个家伙塞进烤箱里的情景分心了。抱歉。

00:23:06.640 --> 00:23:07.810
<v Sean Cole>What? What?
<v Sean Cole>什么？什么？

00:23:07.810 --> 00:23:10.030
<v B.A. Parker>No, that's the Underground Railroad. It's a fake oven.
<v B.A. Parker>不，那是地下铁路。那是个假烤箱。

00:23:10.030 --> 00:23:11.390
<v Sean Cole>Oh, OK.
<v Sean Cole>哦，好吧。

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:22.100
<v B.A. Parker>There was an exhibit behind Sean of a white man helping his runaway slave escape through an oven with a door in the back, allowing him to slip through from one house to another and another.
<v B.A. Parker>肖恩身后有一个展览，展示了一个白人男子帮助他逃跑的奴隶通过一个后面有门的烤箱逃生，让他从一个房子溜到另一个房子，再到另一个房子。

00:23:22.100 --> 00:23:36.480
<v B.A. Parker>As a kid, it wasn't like I was unfamiliar with Black history. By the third grade, I had already been to the plantation in North Carolina where my ancestors were sold. I was obsessed with Thurgood Marshall. I wanted to be him. I wanted to marry him, everything.
<v B.A. Parker>作为一个孩子，我对黑人历史并不是不熟悉。到三年级时，我已经去过北卡罗来纳州的种植园，我的祖先就是在那里被卖掉的。我迷恋瑟古德·马歇尔。我想成为他。我想嫁给他，一切。

00:23:36.480 --> 00:24:01.200
<v B.A. Parker>And my elementary school made such a big deal about Martin Luther King every year. And I once got to play one of Coretta Scott King's bridesmaids in an assembly. But it was exactly that, the elementary school version of Black history-- Dr. King, Nelson Mandela, Rosa Parks, the coloring book of greatest hits, not the brutal reality.
<v B.A. Parker>我的小学每年都对马丁·路德·金大做文章。我有一次在集会上扮演科雷塔·斯科特·金的一个伴娘。但正是这一点，黑人历史的小学版本——金博士、纳尔逊·曼德拉、罗莎·帕克斯，最伟大热门歌曲的填色书，而不是残酷的现实。

00:24:01.200 --> 00:24:15.360
<v B.A. Parker>But none of this compares to what you see in the museum's basement. The basement is so intense that the museum has posted a sign outside of it, warning that you shouldn't go into it until you're at least 12. Under 16, you need a chaperone.
<v B.A. Parker>但所有这些都无法与你在博物馆地下室看到的情景相比。地下室太震撼了，以至于博物馆在外面贴了一个标志，警告说你至少12岁才能进去。16岁以下需要有成人陪同。

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:32.280
<v B.A. Parker>We headed there, past Henry Box Brown, the slave who mailed himself to freedom in a box. When I was a kid, he'd pop out and wave. But it startled too many kids, and they don't do that anymore. We walked past Henry to the top of the basement stairs.
<v B.A. Parker>我们朝那边走去，走过了亨利·箱子·布朗，那个把自己装在箱子里寄向自由的奴隶。当我还是个孩子的时候，他会跳出来挥手。但它吓到了太多孩子，他们不再那样做了。我们走过亨利，来到地下室台阶的顶部。

00:24:32.280 --> 00:24:39.150
<v B.A. Parker>All right, so we're going to go downstairs to the lynching exhibit.
<v B.A. Parker>好吧，所以我们要下楼去看看林奇（私刑）展览。

00:24:39.150 --> 00:24:49.920
<v Ira Glass>I didn't see the lynching exhibit when I was eight. I saw it on a second field trip, when I was a freshman in high school. I still don't think I was ready. I don't think I was ready now.
<v Ira Glass>我八岁时没有看到林奇展览。我是在第二次实地考察时看到的，那时我上高一。我仍然认为我还没有准备好。我想我现在也没有准备好。

00:24:49.920 --> 00:25:20.970
<v Ira Glass>It starts off when you're still walking down the stairs, with historical photos and newspaper clippings of actual lynchings as recent as 1998. Black people hanging from trees, set on fire, dragged by trucks. Photos of the dead surrounded by crowds of white spectators all decked out in their Sunday best. Smiling little girls with the ribbons in their hair, looking at the camera, some with pride, a man hanging from a tree behind them. Then at the bottom of the stairs, as soon as you round the corner--
<v Ira Glass>当你还在走下台阶时，它就开始了，展示了真实林奇的历史照片和报纸剪报，最近的甚至到1998年。黑人被吊在树上、放火烧死、被卡车拖拽。死者周围挤满了盛装打扮的白人旁观者的照片。梳着蝴蝶结、对着镜头微笑着的小女孩，有的人带着自豪，他们身后的树上挂着一个男人。然后在台阶底部，一拐过拐角——

00:25:20.970 --> 00:25:21.810
<v B.A. Parker>All right, so--
<v B.A. Parker>好吧，所以——

00:25:21.810 --> 00:25:23.180
<v Sean Cole>Oh, boy.
<v Sean Cole>哦，天哪。

00:25:23.180 --> 00:25:24.330
<v B.A. Parker>Yeah.
<v B.A. Parker>是的。

00:25:24.330 --> 00:25:28.010
<v Sean Cole>Oh. Oh, my God.
<v Sean Cole>哦。哦，天哪。

00:25:28.010 --> 00:25:31.770
<v B.A. Parker>Do you want me to describe what's happening here?
<v B.A. Parker>你想让我描述这里发生的事情吗？

00:25:31.770 --> 00:25:50.640
<v Ira Glass>In front of us was a husband and wife named Hayes and Mary Turner. They lived in Georgia. Back in 1918, a local Black man had killed the plantation owner. And when the Sheriff's Office went hunting for the killer, they and a mob lynched practically every other Black man in their path.
<v Ira Glass>在我们面前是一对名为海斯和玛丽·特纳的夫妇。他们住在佐治亚州。早在1918年，当地一个黑人杀害了种植园主。当治安官办公室搜寻凶手时，他们和暴徒几乎对路上遇到的每一个黑人男子实施了私刑。

00:25:50.640 --> 00:26:06.720
<v Ira Glass>Hayes Turner was one of the casualties in that massacre-- a warning, this gets especially graphic. Just as I was about to describe what happened next to Sean, the museum's educator, Imani Haynes, walked in. And I was grateful she did, because she took over.
<v Ira Glass>海斯·特纳是那场大屠杀中的死难者之一——一个警告，这会变得格外血腥。就在我准备向肖恩描述接下来发生的事情时，博物馆的教育工作者伊马尼·海恩斯走了进来。我很感激她进来了，因为她接管了。

00:26:06.720 --> 00:26:29.360
<v Imani Haynes>He had nothing to do with the situation, and unfortunately, he was lynched. Afterwards, he was castrated. So what you're seeing in this scene in the exhibit is Turner, lynched. He is wearing overalls, and we have his overalls opened up in his private region, blood dripping down.
<v Imani Haynes>他与那场情况毫无关系，不幸的是，他被私刑处死了。之后，他被阉割了。所以你在展览的这个场景中看到的是，特纳被施以私刑。他穿着工装裤，我们把他的工装裤在隐私部位剪开，鲜血直流。

00:26:29.360 --> 00:26:41.400
<v B.A. Parker>Next to Hayes is his wife, Mary. After he was lynched, Mary bravely went seeking justice for her husband. She was eight months pregnant at the time. They lynched her too.
<v B.A. Parker>海斯旁边是他的妻子玛丽。在他被私刑处死后，玛丽勇敢地为她的丈夫寻求正义。当时她已经怀孕八个月了。他们也对她实施了私刑。

00:26:41.400 --> 00:26:57.300
<v B.A. Parker>In the museum exhibit, she's hanging from the neck. But in real life, they actually hanged her by her feet, set her on fire, slashed her stomach open, and pulled out her baby, which was still alive. That's what's depicted in wax. Then they stomp on its head.
<v B.A. Parker>在博物馆的展览中，她是脖子挂着吊死的。但在现实生活中，他们实际上把她倒挂双脚吊起来，放火烧她，划开她的胃，把她的婴儿掏出来，当时婴儿还活着。这就是蜡像中所描绘的。然后他们踩碎了它的头。

00:26:57.300 --> 00:27:24.770
<v B.A. Parker>The sign on the wall adds to the story that haunted me. It's also the only part that historians can't fully verify. It reads, "Walter White, describing the lynching of Mary Turner and her husband, Hayes Turner, depicted in the scene, said that it was too horrible to describe the mob taking the time to sew two cats in Mrs. Turner's stomach and making bets as to which one would climb out first."
<v B.A. Parker>墙上的标语增添了这个一直困扰我的故事。这也是历史学家无法完全核实的部分。上面写着：“沃尔特·怀特在描述场景中描绘的玛丽·特纳和她的丈夫海斯·特纳受私刑的过程时说，暴徒花时间把两只猫缝进特纳夫人的胃里并打赌哪一只会先爬出来，这太可怕了，无法形容。”

00:27:24.770 --> 00:27:48.770
<v B.A. Parker>To the left of the Turner's bodies is a mantelpiece with souvenir jars. Sometimes, the lynchers would keep pieces of their victims. In these jars were supposed to be the ears, locks of hair, and genitalia of Black people, all next to a kid's baseball trophy. I'm not sure why that's there, except to underscore the point that these body parts are trophies too.
<v B.A. Parker>特纳夫妇尸体的左侧是一个放着纪念罐的壁炉架。有时，施刑者会保留受害者的身体部位。据称，这些罐子里装着黑人的耳朵、一束束头发和生殖器，所有这些都紧挨着一个孩子的棒球奖杯。我不确定为什么那个奖杯会在那里，除非是为了强调这些身体部位也是战利品。

00:27:48.770 --> 00:28:05.180
<v B.A. Parker>It's a difficult room to process. When I saw this at 14, some of my classmates didn't even know how to react. A few of them, stoic. Others, awkwardly deflecting with, yo. I remember feeling angry, because you couldn't escape it.
<v B.A. Parker>这是一个很难消化的房间。当我14岁看到这一幕时，我的一些同学甚至不知道该作何反应。他们中的几个人表现得很坚忍。其他人则尴尬地用“哟”来转移话题。我记得当时感到很愤怒，因为你无处可逃。

00:28:05.180 --> 00:28:22.470
<v B.A. Parker>There are signs around the room that anticipate your distress. They say, "Identify with the victims and martyrs, and never forget them. But do not get bitter or despondent over what they endured."
<v B.A. Parker>房间四周有些标语，预示着你会感到痛苦。上面写着：“认同受害者和烈士，永远不要忘记他们。但不要对他们所承受的苦难感到痛苦或绝望。”

00:28:22.470 --> 00:28:37.590
<v B.A. Parker>Dr. Joanne Martin founded the museum with her husband, Elmer, in 1983. And when I told her all the kids in my third grade were crying and freaking out and holding hands, she was fully aware that happens. In fact, it's intentional.
<v B.A. Parker>乔安妮·马丁博士于1983年与她的丈夫埃尔默共同创立了这个博物馆。当我告诉她我三年级的所有孩子都在哭泣、惊慌失措并手拉着手时，她完全知道这种情况会发生。事实上，这是故意安排的。

00:28:37.590 --> 00:29:26.430
<v Joanne Martin>For me, that's the point. When they leave shaking, when they leave crying-- and that's often the reaction-- I have no problem with those tears. And it is-- what people are going to talk about years later is going to be our slave ship. And they're going to say, I never forgot your lynching exhibit. And everything else, it seems like a movie, if you don't have a sense of exactly what people were fighting against. If you think that Rosa Parks-- that what she did had something to do with the seat on the bus, and you don't understand that that act was going to get her jailed, but it could have gotten her lynched as well.
<v Joanne Martin>对我来说，这才是重点。当他们浑身颤抖地离开，当他们哭泣着离开——这通常是他们的反应——我对这些眼泪没有任何异议。而且——人们多年后会谈论的将是我们的奴隶船。他们会说，我永远不会忘记你们的林奇（私刑）展览。而其他的一切，如果你不确切了解人们当时在对抗什么，看起来就像是一部电影。如果你认为罗莎·帕克斯——她所做的事与公交车座位有关，而你不明白那种行为会让她入狱，但也可能让她被私刑处死。

00:29:26.430 --> 00:29:50.910
<v B.A. Parker>When Joanne and her husband founded the museum, they set out to create something that they wished existed in the world, a museum that celebrated and preserved Black history, created by Black people for Black people in a majority Black city. They were professors at historically Black universities in Baltimore, Elmer at Morgan State and Joanne at Coppin. But they wanted to reach more than just the academic class.
<v B.A. Parker>当乔安妮和她的丈夫创立博物馆时，他们着手创造一些他们希望世界上存在的东西——一个由黑人创造、为黑人服务、位于黑人占多数的城市的、庆祝并保存黑人历史的博物馆。他们曾是巴尔的摩历史悠久的黑人大学的教授，埃尔默在摩根州立大学，乔安妮在科宾大学。但他们的目标不仅仅是学术阶层。

00:29:50.910 --> 00:30:04.740
<v B.A. Parker>When they visited a wax museum in St. Augustine, Florida, they thought, yeah, wax figures. That's the way to go. They had money saved for a house, but instead put it towards wax figures. It still wasn't enough.
<v B.A. Parker>当他们参观佛罗里达州圣奥古斯丁的一家蜡像馆时，他们想，对，蜡像。就选这个。他们原本存了买房子的钱，但转而把它投在了蜡像上。这仍然不够。

00:30:04.740 --> 00:30:27.080
<v B.A. Parker>Wax figures are absurdly expensive-- $4,500 then, but tens of thousands of dollars now. They ended up with their first four Black history figures, Frederick Douglass, John Brown, Nat Turner, and Mary McLeod Bethune. Elmer and Joanne would tour around with their quartet of wax figures at different schools and churches and expos.
<v B.A. Parker>蜡像贵得离谱——当时要4500美元，现在则要几万美元。他们最终得到了最初的四位黑人历史人物：弗雷德里克·道格拉斯、约翰·布朗、纳特·特纳和玛丽·麦克劳德·白求恩。埃尔默和乔安妮会带着他们的四尊蜡像四处巡展，去往不同的学校、教堂和博览会。

00:30:27.080 --> 00:30:58.290
<v Joanne Martin>We just would take the figures, set them up as exhibits, throw them into my Pontiac, take them back home to our two-bedroom apartment. So if you looked into the guest bedroom of our two-bedroom apartment, you would have found Mary McLeod Bethune's head on the dresser and Frederick Douglass' parts over in a corner, and so forth, You know, I just gave him an ultimatum. Mary McLeod Bethune has got to go. You've got to find a place for Mary McLeod Bethune.
<v Joanne Martin>我们只是把蜡像带上，搭建成展览，把它们扔进我的庞蒂亚克汽车里，带回我们两居室的公寓。所以如果你往我们两居室公寓的客房里看，你会发现玛丽·麦克劳德·白求恩的头放在梳妆台上，弗雷德里克·道格拉斯的身体部件在角落里，诸如此类。你知道的，我向他下了最后通牒。玛丽·麦克劳德·白求恩必须离开。你必须为玛丽·麦克劳德·白求恩找个地方。

00:30:58.290 --> 00:31:15.230
<v B.A. Parker>Dr. Martin told me they're thinking of expanding the museum and making it more child friendly, toning down the violence, which surprised me. It doesn't seem like her mission at all, and it only makes sense if our history were a little more child friendly, which it definitely isn't.
<v B.A. Parker>马丁博士告诉我，他们正考虑扩建博物馆，让它对儿童更友好一些，淡化暴力，这让我感到惊讶。这看起来一点也不符合她的使命，只有当我们的历史再对儿童友好一点时才说得通，而它显然一点也不友好。

00:31:15.230 --> 00:31:37.360
<v B.A. Parker>The four little girls killed in the 1963 church bombing in Alabama, they were all 14 and younger. The kids who marched in Birmingham's Children's Crusade were beaten and had dogs sicced on them. They were as young as 12. And Emmett Till was 14. He has a memorial in the lynching room, but you wouldn't even be allowed to go down there without a chaperone.
<v B.A. Parker>1963年阿拉巴马州教堂爆炸案中遇害的那四个小女孩，当时都只有14岁或更小。在伯明翰儿童游行中游行的孩子们遭到殴打，并被恶犬扑咬。他们中最小的只有12岁。而埃米特·蒂尔只有14岁。他在私刑展厅里有一个纪念碑，但如果没有成年人陪同，你甚至不被允许下到那个房间里去。

00:31:37.360 --> 00:32:09.970
<v B.A. Parker>Even though the museum upset me, I respect it. It got through to me. Even as an adult watching right-wing pundits discuss the greatness of America, I still think of Mary Turner and her baby. When I left the museum, I have the same feeling I did as a kid, like I was in on a secret about what the country was really like, like all of America's truths were in a basement on North Avenue.
<v B.A. Parker>尽管这个博物馆让我感到不安，但我很尊敬它。它触动了我。甚至作为一个成年人，当看到右翼评论员讨论美国的伟大时，我仍然会想起玛丽·特纳和她的孩子。当我离开博物馆时，我有一种和儿时一样的感觉——就像我知道这个国家真实面貌的秘密一样，就像美国所有的真相都藏在北大道的一个地下室里。

00:32:09.970 --> 00:32:30.370
<v Ira Glass>B.A. Parker-- she can be heard on the podcast "The Cut" from "New York Magazine." Coming up, a seventh grader gets a taste of the family business, which means breaking the law with mom and dad. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>B·A·帕克——你可以在《纽约客》杂志的播客《The Cut》中听到她的声音。接下来，一个七年级学生初尝家族生意的滋味，这意味着要和爸爸妈妈一起违法。一会儿我们将从芝加哥公共广播电台继续播出。

00:32:30.370 --> 00:32:45.830
<v Ira Glass>It's "This American Life." I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, Suitable for Children, stories about the random stuff that kids encounter that just sticks with them for the rest of their lives, for good or for bad. We have arrived at Act Three of our program. Act Three, Take Your Kid to Work Day.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《这个美国生活》。我是伊拉·格拉斯。今天的节目是“适合儿童”，讲述孩子们偶然遇到的一些事情，这些事情会伴随他们一生，无论好坏。我们来到了节目的第三幕。第三幕，带你的孩子去上班日。

00:32:45.830 --> 00:33:01.270
<v Ira Glass>So in this half of the show, we have stories of kids seeing stuff that really maybe they should not see, and we begin with this story from Domingo Martinez about tagging along with his parents when they were on the job. His dad had a trucking business in Texas. They were doing worse and worse over the years.
<v Ira Glass>所以在节目的这一半中，我们将讲述孩子们看到一些他们也许真不该看的东西的故事，我们从多明戈·马丁内斯的这个故事开始，讲述他跟着父母去上班的情景。他的爸爸在得克萨斯州经营一家卡车运输业务。这些年来，他们的生意每况愈下。

00:33:01.270 --> 00:33:19.640
<v Domingo Martinez>By 1986, Dad had become a truck driver with nothing left to haul but marijuana. Through his early 30s, he could do little with the trucking business he'd inherited from Grandpa, except watch as it crumbled around him. Back then, he began his days by throwing open the door to the bedroom I shared with my brother Dan.
<v Domingo Martinez>到1986年，爸爸成了一名卡车司机，车上除了大麻之外什么也拉不了。在他30岁出头的年纪，对于从爷爷那里继承来的卡车运输业务，他什么也做不了，只能眼睁睁地看着它在自己周围崩塌。那时，他每天都是从猛然推开我和弟弟丹共用的卧室门开始的。

00:33:19.640 --> 00:33:40.680
<v Domingo Martinez>He'd stand there in his underwear, looking like a tall, diapered child, and say, "You two, get up." He would end the day drinking a lot of $1.50 Budweisers at the shanty bars that littered the port of Brownsville. At home, Mom kept the bookkeeping. She watched the flow of money slow to a trickle, and then stop outright when I was in middle school.
<v Domingo Martinez>他会穿着内裤站在那里，看起来像个高大的、包着尿布的孩子，然后说：“你们两个，起来。”他的一天往往会在布朗斯维尔港口周围星罗棋布的破落酒吧里喝掉大量1.5美元的百威啤酒中结束。在家里，妈妈负责记账。她看着资金流逐渐变成涓涓细流，并在我上初中时彻底断流。

00:33:40.680 --> 00:33:59.510
<v Domingo Martinez>My brother, sisters, and I knew things were dire when our mothers stopped shopping at El Centro Supermarket-- the nice grocery store, as she'd always told us-- and started bringing home bags from Lopez Supermarket, the poor people grocery store.
<v Domingo Martinez>我和弟弟妹妹们知道情况已经到了绝境，是从妈妈不再去埃尔门特罗超市——她总是告诉我们那是一家很好的杂货店——而是开始提着洛佩兹超市的袋子回家开始的。洛佩兹超市是穷人去的杂货店。

00:33:59.510 --> 00:34:20.290
<v Domingo Martinez>One morning, mom surprises me by showing up at my seventh grade algebra class and removing me, getting us on the road out of Brownsville, headed north. There's no explanation. "Sientáte y lla callete," she snaps at me. Sit down, and shut up.
<v Domingo Martinez>一天早晨，妈妈出现在我七年级代数课的教室里，把我叫了出去，带我们踏上了离开布朗斯维尔、向北行驶的公路，这让我大吃一惊。没有任何解释。“Sientáte y lla callete，”她冲我厉声说道。坐下，闭嘴。

00:34:20.290 --> 00:34:47.140
<v Domingo Martinez>I don't know why I didn't guess what we were doing that morning, that Mom and I were lookouts for Dad, who was on the road somewhere behind us in one of his flat-nosed tractor trailers, carrying a large load of marijuana and headed north. I had heard the stories, knew some of the tactics of smuggling by this point. But I didn't make the connection that mom and I were driving ahead of Dad to make sure the customs station was closed. And if it happened to be open, we were supposed to turn back and warn him.
<v Domingo Martinez>我不知道为什么那天早上我没有猜到我们在做什么，没有猜到妈妈和我是在为爸爸把风——爸爸开着他的平头拖挂式卡车拉着一大车大麻正在我们身后的某条路上向北行驶。我听说过那些故事，到那时已经了解了一些走私的战术。但我没有把我们母子俩开车走在爸爸前面以确保海关检查站关闭这件事联系起来。如果恰好开着，我们本该掉头警告他。

00:34:47.140 --> 00:35:04.330
<v Domingo Martinez>This was back in the 1980s, before the Patriot Act, when there were two US customs checkpoints blocking the migration of drugs, fruit, people, reptiles, and parrots on the roads between the United States and Mexico, both about 100 miles north of the Mexican border at highway checkpoints.
<v Domingo Martinez>那是20世纪80年代，在《爱国者法案》出台之前，当时美国和墨西哥之间的公路上有两个美国海关检查站，用来阻断毒品、水果、人口、爬行动物和鹦鹉的迁移，这两个检查站都在距离墨西哥边境以北约100英里的公路检查站上。

00:35:04.330 --> 00:35:26.560
<v Domingo Martinez>The station on Highway 77 in Sarita was the busier and the better financed. The checkpoint in Hebbronville, though, was an Airstream trailer with an attached carport to protect the agents from the sun, and it would often close for breakfast or lunch. So we were headed towards that one this morning, going north on Highway 281, driving through the scenery of asthmatic plants and stunted trees.
<v Domingo Martinez>萨里塔77号公路上的检查站更繁忙、资金也更充足。不过，赫布龙维尔的检查站是一个“银色流光”房车，带有一个附属的车棚，用来保护特工免受阳光照射，它经常在吃早饭或午饭时关闭。所以今天早上我们正开往那个检查站，沿着281号公路向北行驶，穿过长满哮喘植物和矮树的风景区。

00:35:26.560 --> 00:35:37.900
<v Domingo Martinez>The car is hot, no air conditioning. My mom and I are saying nothing to one another. We have each decided that the other is unworthy of conversation. We stare out the windows and think very different things.
<v Domingo Martinez>车里很热，没有空调。我和妈妈一言不发。我们都认定对方不值得跟自己说话。我们凝视着窗外，心里想着截然不同的事情。

00:35:37.900 --> 00:35:42.520
<v Domingo Martinez>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Domingo Martinez>[音乐播放]

00:35:42.520 --> 00:36:09.680
<v Domingo Martinez>Mom is thinking about the $100 she and Dad had spent before six o'clock that morning. $100 that would help them make $2,000 if things went right, because their first stop on this felony excursion was to Dad's curandera, whom we knew as La Senora, Dad's personal witchdoctor, for an emergency session. They were let right through at 5:00 in the morning, and they had only one burning question-- will the checkpoint be open?
<v Domingo Martinez>妈妈正在想着她和爸爸那天清晨6点前花掉的那100美元。这100美元如果一切顺利的话，能帮他们赚到2000美元，因为他们这次重罪之旅的第一站是去找爸爸的库兰德拉（民间治疗师/巫医），我们称她为“夫人”（La Senora），也就是爸爸的私人巫医，进行紧急咨询。他们在早上5点被直接放行，他们只有一个迫切的问题——检查站会开吗？

00:36:09.680 --> 00:36:38.400
<v Domingo Martinez>After years of sitting through these sessions with my grandmother, I didn't have to be there to know exactly how it went in the curandera's office. La Senora is older, matronly, dresses in a thin frock with her hair pulled back in a bun. She has my parents sit across from her desk in a black leather love seat with chrome handles. A panorama of photos hang around her office, some wallet sized, others larger and in portrait. A pencil sketch of a Camaro by one of her grandsons hangs next to a window.
<v Domingo Martinez>在陪着外祖母经历过多年的这类咨询后，我不用到场也确切知道库兰德拉的办公室里是怎么回事。“夫人”年纪较大，有妇人风度，穿着一件薄连衣裙，头发在脑后梳成一个发髻。她让我的父母坐在她办公桌对面一张带镀铬扶手的黑色皮革双人沙发上。她的办公室周围挂着一幅幅照片，有些是钱包大小的，另一些较大且是竖版肖像。她孙子画的一幅卡迈罗轿车的铅笔素描挂在窗户旁边。

00:36:38.400 --> 00:36:51.330
<v Domingo Martinez>She listens closely to their question, nodding sleepily at their preoccupation, sympathizing, understanding. They've reached a decision and they can't turn back, they tell her. They need to do it. No other choice anymore.
<v Domingo Martinez>她密切倾听他们的问题，对他们的忧虑困惑地频频点头，表示同情和理解。他们已经做出决定，而且无法回头了，他们告诉她。他们必须这么做。再也没有别的选择了。

00:36:51.330 --> 00:37:20.170
<v Domingo Martinez>She stands up abruptly and walks behind her desk, which is cluttered with sheets of notebook paper crawling with ink, illegible notes, names of people, sets of cryptic numbers, home addresses of saints. She closes her eyes and scribbles something on her yellow legal pad-- nonsense to anyone else reading it. On her desk are two cheap, leaded crystal bottles, each filled with a clear liquid. She gently reaches for her matching shot glass and lifts it to the light, making it sparkle.
<v Domingo Martinez>她突然站起身，走到办公桌后面。桌上凌乱地摆放着爬满字迹的笔记本纸张、字迹潦草的笔记、人名、神秘的数字组合、圣人的家庭住宅地址。她闭上眼睛，在黄色的法律记事本上乱涂乱画——对任何其他人来说都是毫无意义的胡话。她的桌上放着两个便宜的含铅水晶瓶，每个瓶子里都装着一种清澈的液体。她轻轻拿起与之一套的小酒杯，把它举向光线中，让它闪闪发光。

00:37:20.170 --> 00:37:50.730
<v Domingo Martinez>She places the glass on the surface before her and pours from one bottle, then the other. When the two clear liquids mix, they turn a deep crimson and thicken, like plasma. She nods her head as if her suspicions have been confirmed. She produces a glass orb filled with water sitting on a black, plastic ring. She sits up straight, closes her eyes, and regulates her breathing into a loud, rhythmic inhalation, exhalation. That unconsciously forces Mom and Dad to do the same.
<v Domingo Martinez>她把杯子放在面前的桌面上，从一个瓶子里倒液体，然后从另一个瓶子里倒。当这两种清澈的液体混合在一起时，它们变成了深红色并变得浓稠，就像血浆一样。她点点头，仿佛她的疑虑得到了证实。她拿出一个装着水、放在黑色塑料环上的玻璃球。她挺直身子，闭上眼睛，将呼吸调整为大声而有节奏的吸气和呼气。这在潜意识里迫使爸爸妈妈也这样做。

00:37:50.730 --> 00:38:14.070
<v Domingo Martinez>After a few seconds, she opens her eyes suddenly and strikes the glass orb with a metal wand, making it ring in the clear morning air. Then she lifts the orb between her two pudgy hands and stares deeply into the inverted image of the room around it. Sitting opposite the chicanery and watching everything she does, you're drawn to the ritual. And you can't help but try to figure it out too.
<v Domingo Martinez>几秒钟后，她突然睁开眼睛，用一根金属魔杖敲击玻璃球，让它在清澈的早晨空气中发出清脆的响声。然后她用两只胖乎乎的手捧起玻璃球，深深地凝视着周围房间在球中的倒影。坐在骗局的对面，看着她所做的一切，你会被这种仪式吸引。而且你也不禁会去琢磨它。

00:38:14.070 --> 00:38:29.300
<v Domingo Martinez>No, she says with certainty. The checkpoint will not be open this morning. Go in peace. God be with you.
<v Domingo Martinez>“不，”她笃定地说道。“今天早上检查站不会开。安心去吧。上帝与你们同在。”

00:38:29.300 --> 00:38:47.080
<v Domingo Martinez>When Mom and I reached the checkpoint at 11:45 that morning, it is very much open for business. A line of cars five deep stretches back from the under-budgeted Airstream trailer. Our job now is to go through, and then turn back around and report to dad that his doom was indeed imminent.
<v Domingo Martinez>那天上午11点45分，当我和妈妈到达检查站时，它完全开着营业。从经费不足的“银色流光”房车开始，排起了五辆车深的汽车长龙。我们现在的任务是穿过去，然后掉头回来向爸爸报告说，他的厄运确实迫在眉睫。

00:38:47.080 --> 00:39:06.790
<v Domingo Martinez>Mom is visibly shaken. She turns off the radio with a hard click and considers this, considers her options. From the car, we can see a skinny, middle-aged white guy in green Border Patrol garb, sitting on a reclining chair in the open doorway of the Airstream trailer. He's facing the cars as they pass under the tin roof.
<v Domingo Martinez>妈妈明显受到了惊吓。她“啪”的一声用力关掉收音机，陷入沉思，权衡着自己的选择。从车里我们可以看到，一个穿着绿色边防巡逻队制服的瘦削中年白人男子正坐在“银色流光”房车敞开的门口的躺椅上。他正面对着从铁皮屋顶下驶过的汽车。

00:39:06.790 --> 00:39:34.080
<v Domingo Martinez>It must have been 110 degrees inside the trailer, 100 in the shade. From his position, never bothering to get up, the agent sits, fanning himself with the newspaper and sleepily peers into the cars, asking if everyone is an American citizen, waiting for an accent and answer, smiling, and then waving them through, like a sympathetic priest granting absolution. "Hi. Where are y'all going to today?" asks the skinny, little man.
<v Domingo Martinez>房车里的温度一定有110华氏度（约43摄氏度），树荫下也有100华氏度。这位特工坐在那里，始终懒得站起来，用报纸扇着风，睡眼惺忪地往车里张望，询问每个人是否都是美国公民，等待着带有口音的回答，微笑着，然后挥手放行，就像一个给予救赎的慈悲神父。“嗨。你们今天打算去哪儿？”那个瘦小的男人问。

00:39:34.080 --> 00:39:50.880
<v Domingo Martinez>Mom's window is now only halfway open, and in her nervousness, she rolls it all the way up. "Hi, sorry. I mean, San Antonio," as she opens a window. "Y'all American citizens?" "Yes," she says, as she leans back so he could get a clear look at me, nodding my head.
<v Domingo Martinez>妈妈的车窗现在只摇下来了一半，由于紧张，她把车窗全摇上去了。“嗨，不好意思。我是说，圣安东尼奥，”她说着摇下车窗。“你们都是美国公民吗？”“是的，”她说着往后靠，好让他能看清我，我点着头。

00:39:50.880 --> 00:40:07.800
<v Domingo Martinez>I don't say anything, except a quiet "Yup" when I nod, but he has not heard me. There is an uncomfortable pause-- I think because they're supposed to hear your accent. There was an anxious beat, and he still hasn't let us go when I say, "Yes, I am," a bit too enthusiastically.
<v Domingo Martinez>我什么也没说，只是在点头时轻声说了句“对”，但他并没有听到。现场出现了一段令人尴尬的停顿——我想是因为他们本该听到你的口音。空气中弥漫着焦虑的节拍，而当我们放行时他依然没有放行，于是我有点过于热情地说道：“是的，我是。”

00:40:07.800 --> 00:40:30.640
<v Domingo Martinez>Mom laughs nervously. She had been hoping our light skin would exonerate us without a problem. "Y'all have a nice day," he says, like he hasn't noticed anything awkward. We drive through, and I feel her seethe for the next few miles.
<v Domingo Martinez>妈妈紧张地笑了起来。她一直希望我们较浅的肤色能让我们免于麻烦。“祝你们过愉快的一天，”他说，仿佛他没有注意到任何尴尬。我们开车穿了过去，在接下来的几英里里，我能感觉到她在暗自怒火中烧。

00:40:30.640 --> 00:41:08.570
<v Domingo Martinez>That was about noon. Somewhere behind us at a roadside rest stop, Dad and my older brother, Dan, are waiting in Dad's tractor trailer, which is attached to the trailer with the marijuana. The two center I-beams that run the length of the trailer make perfect housing once you weld metal plates all along the underside and at the tail end. It creates a long, rectangular box sealed at the rear end with the axle of the rig, where 20 40-pound blocks of marijuana can be hidden. This is what Dad and Dan are carrying.
<v Domingo Martinez>那时大约是中午。在我们身后的某个路边休息区，爸爸和我哥哥丹正坐在爸爸的牵引式卡车里，卡车后面挂着装有大麻的拖车。贯穿拖车全长的两根中心工字梁，一旦你沿着底面和尾端焊接好金属板，就能构成完美的藏身之所。它形成了一个长方形的箱子，在后部由卡车的车轴密封，里面可以藏20个40磅重的大麻砖。这就是爸爸和丹运载的东西。

00:41:08.570 --> 00:41:29.270
<v Domingo Martinez>I wonder dimly if Dan has figured out what he's doing out here on the road north, when he should be in high school. But Dan has always been more streetwise, much savvier than me. I figure that if I figured it out, he'd have figured it out long before.
<v Domingo Martinez>我隐隐在想，丹是否已经明白他在本该上高中的年纪却在这里的大道上做什么。但丹一直比我更懂人情世故，比我精明得多。我想，既然我都看出来了，他肯定早就看出来了。

00:41:29.270 --> 00:41:38.720
<v Domingo Martinez>Mom and I drove on to Hebbronville. I'm dehydrated. I want to stop at a store. We can't, she says to me. We have to turn back. I think we're behind.
<v Domingo Martinez>我和妈妈开车继续前往赫布龙维尔。我很脱水。我想在商店停一下。我们不能停，她对我说。我们得掉头。我觉得我们落后了。

00:41:38.720 --> 00:41:50.540
<v Domingo Martinez>Still, I'm hungry and really thirsty. So I persist. Certainly, we have time to get to a convenience store. They can't have scheduled this thing to hinge on a few minutes. Certainly, they've devised this more cleverly than that.
<v Domingo Martinez>尽管如此，我又饿又渴。所以我坚持着。我们肯定有时间去便利店。他们不可能把这事安排得只差这几分钟。他们肯定把计划设计得比这更巧妙。

00:41:50.540 --> 00:42:10.220
<v Domingo Martinez>Please, I plead, just stop at the 7-Eleven. Grudgingly, she pilots the noisy car around the small town and finds a convenience store. I get out with a couple of dollars and come up short when I tried paying for some donuts and a chocolate milk. I returned to the car for more money. Just get in, she yells, and I do, leaving the food at the counter.
<v Domingo Martinez>求求你，我央求道，就在7-Eleven停一下。她不情愿地开着那辆吵闹的车绕过小镇，找到了一家便利店。我拿着几美元下了车，但当我试图支付一些甜甜圈和巧克力奶的费用时，钱不够。我又回到车里去拿钱。“上车就行，”她大喊道，我照做了，把食物留在了柜台上。

00:42:10.220 --> 00:42:32.870
<v Domingo Martinez>Mom whips the car around and I'm in trouble. I'm used to it by now. We travel like that for a few minutes, and when the checkpoint is back in sight, we hold our breath so the guy won't see us. And right then, we see Dad and Dan and the trailer, heading right for the checkpoint.
<v Domingo Martinez>妈妈猛地一打方向盘，我知道我惹上麻烦了。不过我对这阵仗早就习以为常了。我们这样开了几分钟，等检查站重新出现在视野里时，我们都屏住呼吸，免得那家伙看到我们。就在这时，我们看到爸爸、丹和拖车正径直朝检查站开去。

00:42:32.870 --> 00:42:52.190
<v Domingo Martinez>Dad had become nervous, or maybe brave, or maybe he was blinded by his $100 faith in his curandera and had charged forth without waiting for Mom's report. "Oh, god," my mother says, and all the blood drains from my face from just the tone in her voice. I had never heard her voice so charged with fear.
<v Domingo Martinez>爸爸变得紧张起来，也许是变勇敢了，又或者他被自己对那位女巫医的一百美元信仰冲昏了头脑，竟然没等妈妈的汇报就径直冲了上去。“哦，天哪，”我妈说，仅仅从她说话的语调中，我脸上的血色就瞬间褪尽了。我从未听过她的声音里夹杂着如此恐惧的情绪。

00:42:52.190 --> 00:43:29.300
<v Domingo Martinez>"Oh, god," she says again. With her left hand, she grabs the knob that controls the headlights and flashes at Dad, who is only a few hundred feet from the checkpoint. She flashes four times, in clear view of anyone who is paying attention. I wonder if that is her signal that the checkpoint is open, but question the wisdom of doing it while he is next in line, in full view of the guy at the checkpoint, were he to look over his shoulder and see the noisy, southbound car that just drove through not 30 minutes before. She slows down, slows terrifically down, going less than 20 miles per hour on the highway, and for a moment, I can see Dad is driving.
<v Domingo Martinez>“哦，天哪，”她又说了一遍。她用左手抓住控制车头灯的旋钮，向爸爸闪了几下灯，此时爸爸离检查站只有几百英尺远。她闪了四下，任何留心观察的人都能看得清清楚楚。我在想这是否是她发出的检查站畅通的信号，但又怀疑在爸爸离检查站的工作人员仅一步之遥、只要那人一回头就能看到不到三十分钟前才刚轰鸣着开过去的南行汽车的情况下，这么做是否明智。她放慢了速度，慢得离谱，在高速公路上以不到二十英里的时速行驶，有一会儿，我能看到是爸爸在开车。

00:43:29.300 --> 00:44:10.060
<v Domingo Martinez>I can see that the blood is drained from his face too, making his eyebrows stand out a rich black on his pallid, deathly-white forehead, a mask of someone pretending desperately that everything is A-OK, but doing it horrifically badly. I see Dan in the passenger seat, holding onto the handrail above the door as a means to steady himself through this craziness, through this stupid, unnecessary risk. And he looks calm, collected, uncaring, like he isn't there.
<v Domingo Martinez>我能看出爸爸脸上的血色也褪得精光，使得他苍白如死灰的额头上，浓黑的眉毛显得格外突兀。他戴着一副面具，拼命伪装成一切都好得不能再好的样子，却又演得糟糕透顶。我看到丹坐在副驾驶座上，抓着车门上方的扶手，试图在这一场疯狂、毫无必要的冒险中稳住自己。而他看起来却镇定自若、无动于衷，仿佛他根本不在那里一样。

00:44:10.060 --> 00:44:46.450
<v Domingo Martinez>There are no cars ahead of them now. The trailer is next in line. It slows to a crawl, and I could see dad desperately trying to ignore the crazy, flashing, noisy Bonneville rattling through the southbound lane, keeping his eyes fixed to the road, pretending like he isn't smuggling anything in the empty trailer behind him, this empty, useless trailer with the I-beams all sealed up, with the two Mexican men driving it with no obvious destination, no paperwork, no affiliation with any hauling or trucking company. No, sir, not trafficking in drugs, just driving through-- to Houston probably, looking for work.
<v Domingo Martinez>现在他们前面没有别的车了。拖车排在了第一位。车速放慢到了爬行状态，我能看到爸爸拼命想要忽视那辆在南行车道上发出刺耳噪音、疯狂闪烁着大灯、发出嘎吱嘎吱响声的博纳维尔轿车，他的双眼死死盯着前方路面，假装他身后那辆空空如也的拖车里没有走私任何东西——这辆空荡荡、毫无用处的拖车，工字钢全部被密封着，由两个没有明确目的地、没有证件、与任何运输或卡车公司都没有关联的墨西哥人驾驶着。不，长官，我们不是在贩毒，只是在开车穿过——大概去休斯顿找工作吧。

00:44:46.450 --> 00:45:01.780
<v Domingo Martinez>Oh, yes, we're American citizens. That's not why we're nervous. We could prove that. That's nothing. We're nervous because of the pot we're hiding, and because we're only getting $2,000 for risking upwards of 10 years in prison. Isn't that funny? Isn't that just hysterical?
<v Domingo Martinez>哦，是的，我们是美国公民。我们紧张可不是因为这个。我们可以证明这一点。那根本不算什么。我们紧张是因为我们藏了毒，而且为了冒着可能面临长达十年的牢狱之灾的风险，我们只能拿到两千美元。这不是很可笑吗？这难道不让人歇斯底里吗？

00:45:01.780 --> 00:45:44.120
<v Domingo Martinez>But the skinny white guy is having lunch and can't be bothered. In one of the weirder moments of this whole debacle, the Bonneville, the Airstream, and Dad's tractor are all lined up, like some cosmic event. And we see the skinny Border Patrol agent with a white napkin tucked under his chin, and Dad's bloodless face up in the trailer through the window of the Airstream, as the agent waves them through with a fork, like he's conducting an orchestra. Go on through, go on through.
<v Domingo Martinez>但那个瘦瘦的白人男子正在吃午餐，根本没空搭理。在整场闹剧中最诡异的一个瞬间里，那辆博纳维尔轿车、那辆清风房车以及爸爸的拖车排成了一行，宛如某种宇宙奇观。我们透过清风房车的车窗，看到那位下巴上塞着白色餐巾纸的瘦瘦的边境巡逻员，还有拖车里爸爸那张毫无血色的脸，只见那巡逻员手里拿着一把叉子冲他们挥了挥，就像在指挥乐队一样。走吧，走吧，过去吧。

00:45:44.120 --> 00:46:05.940
<v Domingo Martinez>Mom continues driving south, stunned by what has just happened, and I am careful not to say a word. We almost reach Raymondville and stop at a Whataburger to get a quick, wordless lunch, and spend some time sitting in a parking lot, waiting. When she feels it is safe to return, she turns the car around and heads back north, back through the same checkpoint.
<v Domingo Martinez>妈妈继续向南开去，被刚刚发生的一幕惊得说不出话来，我则小心翼翼地一言不发。我们快到雷蒙德维尔时，在一家德克萨斯汉堡店停下来，无言地迅速吃了个午餐，在停车场里坐着等待了一会儿。当她觉得回去安全时，她调转车头向北开去，再次穿过了同一个检查站。

00:46:05.940 --> 00:46:26.670
<v Domingo Martinez>"Are you sure this is safe?" I ask. She doesn't answer. When we pull up again to the checkpoint headed back to Hebbronville around three o'clock, there was a different Border Patrol agent at the Airstream. He is a small, clean-cut, militant Mexican with a southern drawl.
<v Domingo Martinez>“你确定这样安全吗？”我问。她没有回答。大约三点钟，当我们再次把车开到返回赫布隆维尔的检查站时，清风房车旁换了一个不同的边境巡逻员。那是一个身材矮小、精明强干、带点南方口音的激进墨西哥人。

00:46:26.670 --> 00:46:42.550
<v Domingo Martinez>"Y'all American citizens?" he demands instantly, leaning into the car through the window, clearly smelling for marijuana smoke. "Oh, yes," says my mother, who was probably quite relieved the hard part is over and it looks like we're getting away with it. "How about you, son? You an American citizen?"
<v Domingo Martinez>“你们都是美国公民吗？”他立刻质问，把身子探进车窗里，显然在嗅有没有大马哈鱼（注：此处指大麻，原文marijuana smoke）的烟味。“哦，是的，”我妈说，她可能暗自松了一口气，觉得最艰难的部分已经过去，而且看起来我们蒙混过关了。“那你呢，小伙子？你是美国公民吗？”

00:46:42.550 --> 00:46:59.700
<v Domingo Martinez>He gives me a direct glare through reflective sunglasses. There is nothing more potentially hostile than the indigenous ego interpreting the laws of his conqueror upon his own people. "Yes, sir," I say, careful not to move or fidget or look away, like I've learned.
<v Domingo Martinez>他透过反光太阳镜狠狠地瞪了我一眼。没有什么比由本土情歌（注：原意为本土自我/本土土著意识，此处指indigenous ego）在自己同胞身上诠释征服者的法律更具潜在敌意了。“是的，长官，”我说，按照我学到的那样，小心翼翼地不敢乱动、不敢小动作、也不敢移开视线。

00:46:59.700 --> 00:47:16.100
<v Domingo Martinez>He looks back at my mother, then at me, like he smells the nervousness of earlier. Something tugs at his intuition. "Where are you all headed?" the sunglasses finally ask. This catches mom off guard. Her subterfuge had ended when Dad had driven through the checkpoint.
<v Domingo Martinez>他转头看看我妈，又看看我，仿佛嗅到了刚才那种紧张的气息。有什么东西触动了他的直觉。“你们打算去哪儿？”太阳镜最终问道。这让妈妈有些措手不及。她的伪装在爸爸开车通过检查站时就已经结束了。

00:47:16.100 --> 00:47:37.980
<v Domingo Martinez>She left all her answers back in Raymondville. "We're going to Hebbronville," she says unconvincingly. Had he asked one more question, had he pressed it, we would have been in some sort of trouble. But he doesn't, and he backs off and motions us through.
<v Domingo Martinez>她把所有的托词都留在了雷蒙德维尔。“我们要去赫布隆维尔，”她回答得毫无说服力。如果他再多问一个问题，如果他再逼问一下，我们就会惹上某种麻烦。但他没有，他退后一步，挥手示意我们通过。

00:47:37.980 --> 00:48:00.630
<v Domingo Martinez>Mom drives the noisy car through Hebbronville and we were about to hit the open highway again, when we noticed Dad's trailer in the parking of a cheap motel by the side of the road. My brother is lodged between the rearmost tires, working on the brake lights that, ridiculously, aren't working. Mom is as alarmed to find them there as I am. This confirms my suspicion that there had been no planning beyond the consultation at the curandera.
<v Domingo Martinez>妈妈开着那辆噪声轰鸣的车穿过了赫布隆维尔，正准备再次驶入开阔的高速公路时，我们注意到路边一家廉价汽车旅馆的停车场里停着爸爸的拖车。我哥哥正夹在最里面的轮胎中间，修理那些荒谬得根本不亮的刹车灯。妈妈看到他们停在那儿，和我一样感到大惊失色。这证实了我的猜测：除了跟巫医咨询之外，他们根本没有任何计划。

00:48:00.630 --> 00:48:18.150
<v Domingo Martinez>This whole debacle had been based on nothing more than $100 worth of faith. Mom pulls up next to the trailer, and I think she expects a victorious reunion. When he sees her, Dad suddenly looks like he's about to punch her. "Stupid fucking woman!" he yells at her in Spanish. Mom stops cold.
<v Domingo Martinez>这场闹剧完全建立在价值一百美元的信仰之上。妈妈把车停在拖车旁边，我想她是在期待一场胜利的重逢。当爸爸看到她时，突然露出一副要揍她的表情。“蠢透了的臭娘们儿！”他用西班牙语冲她吼道。妈妈僵住了。

00:48:18.150 --> 00:48:30.130
<v Domingo Martinez>"What the fuck were you thinking? If they would have just turned around for a moment, it would all be over, and it would all be your fault," he spits at her in Spanish. We realize what he's upset about-- the flashing headlights.
<v Domingo Martinez>“你他妈脑子里装的是什么？如果他们刚才哪怕回过头去盘问一会儿，一切就都结束了，而且全都是你的错，”他用西班牙语向她啐了一口。我们这才意识到他为什么这么生气——因为闪烁的车头灯。

00:48:30.130 --> 00:48:59.230
<v Domingo Martinez>His words continue to snap and hiss around her like bullets in a firefight, and she follows him forward to the cab of the truck, kowtowing to her punishment. Lucky for us, La Senora was right, Dad says, walking back with Mom, now excited and seemingly over his anger. She made them blind, and we were let through like they were closed.
<v Domingo Martinez>他的话语像交火中的子弹一样继续在她周围劈啪作响、嘶嘶作响，而她顺从地跟着他走到卡车驾驶室前，接受着她的惩罚。幸运的是，西尼奥拉（La Senora）说得对，爸爸说着，和妈妈一起走了回来，此时他已经兴奋起来，似乎气消了。她让巡逻员变成了瞎子，我们就像检查站关门了一样被放行了。

00:48:59.230 --> 00:49:12.700
<v Domingo Martinez>When we left them later that evening, Dan was doing the driving. They pulled out of the parking lot and headed off to Houston and their payoff, the brake lights temporarily fixed in loops of black electrical tape. I watch as the truck disappears.
<v Domingo Martinez>那天傍晚晚些时候我们离开他们时，是丹在开车。他们驶出停车场，朝着休斯顿和他们的酬劳驶去，刹车灯暂时用几圈黑色电工胶带固定好了。我看着卡车渐渐消失。

00:49:12.700 --> 00:49:31.530
<v Domingo Martinez>It looks like every other piece of now broken-down equipment we've ever owned, rusting, miserable, and totally criminal. Mom and I turn south back to Brownsville. Mom is her usual quiet self, except this time she plays the radio louder. This means she's thinking.
<v Domingo Martinez>它看起来就像我们拥有的所有其他破旧设备一样，生锈、悲惨而又彻底透着犯罪的气息。我和妈妈调转车头向南开回布朗斯维尔。妈妈和平时一样安静，不过这次她把收音机声音开得更大了。这意味着她在思考。

00:49:31.530 --> 00:49:45.610
<v Domingo Martinez>It's getting dark, and I'm sleepy, so I sleep. She'll get us home. She knows the way. For that, I could trust her.
<v Domingo Martinez>天渐渐黑了，我很困，于是睡着了。她会带我们回家的。她认识路。为了这一点，我可以信任她。

00:49:45.610 --> 00:49:59.180
<v Ira Glass>Domingo Martinez, reading a story from his collection, "The Boy Kings of Texas," which is a memoir and was a finalist for the National Book Award.
<v Ira Glass>多明戈·马丁内斯（Domingo Martinez），朗读他选集《得克萨斯男孩国王》（The Boy Kings of Texas）中的一个故事。这是一部回忆录，曾入围国家图书奖决赛。

00:49:59.180 --> 00:50:18.170
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Rocket Boy. So we close our program today with somebody who was just a teenager when the Army ordered him to report to Camp Desert Rock, which was next to the Atomic Energy Commission's bomb testing range in Nevada in 1955, to do something that he was totally unprepared for. Though, I think maybe nobody could be prepared for this.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，火箭男孩。因此，我们在今天的节目结尾采访了一位特殊的人物：当军队命令他前往内华达州原子能委员会炸弹试验场附近的沙漠岩石营（Camp Desert Rock）报到时，他才十几岁，当时是1955年。他要去执行一项他完全没有准备好的任务。不过，我想也许没有人能为此做好准备。

00:50:18.170 --> 00:50:39.920
<v Ira Glass>He was 19. His name was Paul Zimmer. He grew up to be a writer and a poet, and he wrote about what happened there. He wrote this account back in the first year of the Trump administration, if you remember, when North Korea was doing test launches of intercontinental missiles and President Trump was threatening retaliation, demanding they stop. Anyway, Paul's account was read for us by an actor, John Conway.
<v Ira Glass>他当时19岁。他的名字叫保罗·齐默（Paul Zimmer）。他后来成为了一名作家和诗人，并写下了那里发生的事情。如果你们还记得的话，这篇自述是他写于特朗普政府执政的第一年，当时朝鲜正在进行洲际导弹试射，特朗普总统则威胁要进行报复，要求他们停止。无论如何，保罗的自述由演员约翰·康威（John Conway）为我们朗读。

00:50:39.920 --> 00:51:02.840
<v John Conway>I had paid attention. I had seen the newsreels of Hiroshima. But just watching atomic bombs go off, I thought it was going to be kind of cool. I thought the story would be a way to interest girls. They didn't tell us anything about what was going to happen, no initiation, no training. And the first time, I had no idea what to expect.
<v John Conway>我当时是很留心的。我见过广岛的纪录片。但仅仅是看着原子弹爆炸，我以为那会是一件挺酷的事。我以为这个故事会成为吸引女孩的一种方式。他们没有告诉我们任何关于将要发生的事情，没有入职仪式，也没有培训。而第一次的时候，我根本不知道会发生什么。

00:51:02.840 --> 00:51:30.810
<v John Conway>We traveled by convoy and buses in the middle of the night to assemble at the site. We shuffled around in the cold, chain smoking, until we were ordered into the trenches. The trenches were long, thin slits in the desert earth only wide enough for us to line up single file. We wore our steel helmets, but we're not issued earplugs, eye covers, or any protective clothing. Then they told us to get down.
<v John Conway>我们在半夜乘车队和公共汽车前往现场集合。我们在寒冷中来回走动，一支接一支地抽着烟，直到我们被命令进入战壕。战壕是沙漠大地上又长又细的裂缝，仅够我们排成单行通过。我们戴着钢制头盔，但没有发给耳塞、眼罩或任何防护服装。接着他们命令我们趴下。

00:51:30.810 --> 00:51:48.510
<v John Conway>I did not become fearful until the countdown was broadcast over the loudspeakers. And I only became terrified when I saw the flash, bright enough where, even with my eyes closed, I could see the bones of my hands over my eyes.
<v John Conway>直到扩音器里广播倒计时的时候，我才开始感到害怕。而当我看到那道强光时，我才真正恐慌起来——那光芒是如此耀眼，以至于即使闭上眼睛，我也能看到自己挡在眼睛前的手上的骨头。

00:51:48.510 --> 00:52:16.110
<v John Conway>A shock wave crashed over the trench top, and we were ordered to stand up and look. We did and saw the mushroom cloud, glowing purple and changing colors, rising and rising up. I saw eight atomic blasts in total, all on different days. Some devices dropped from airplanes, and some detonated from towers. There was an aerial burst and an underground blast as well.
<v John Conway>一股冲击波席卷了战壕顶端，我们被命令站起来观看。我们照做了，看到了蘑菇云，它泛着紫光，变幻着颜色，不断向上升腾。我总共看到了八次原子爆炸，全部在不同的日子。有些装置是从飞机上投下的，有些是从塔上引爆的。其中还有一次空中爆破和一次地下爆破。

00:52:16.110 --> 00:52:39.320
<v John Conway>Sometimes, during the shock wave, the trench sides caved in and buried us alive, so we had to claw our way out from our own graves. Some poor doggies and Jeeps were assigned to drive forward with Geiger counters and radio transmitters. When clearance was radioed back, we were ordered to walk forward into the blast area to bear witness.
<v John Conway>有时，在冲击波的作用下，战壕两侧会坍塌并将我们活埋，所以我们不得不从自己的坟墓里用爪子刨出一条生路。一些可怜的狗狗和吉普车被派去驾驶着盖革计数器和无线电发射器向前侦察。当对讲机里传来放行信号时，我们被命令向前走到爆炸区去见证。

00:52:39.320 --> 00:53:13.770
<v John Conway>As far as I could tell, bearing witness was the entire reason we were there. The largest bomb I witnessed was called Turk, and it was almost three times the size of the Hiroshima bomb. It was dropped from a 500-foot tower, and our trenches were approximately two and a half miles from ground zero. The blast yield was bigger than expected, so the brass ordered us to retreat to the buses, where we swept each other off with kitchen brooms and hustled into our seats.
<v John Conway>据我所知，见证就是我们在那里的全部原因。我亲眼目睹的最大的炸弹被称为“土耳其人”（Turk），其威力几乎是广岛炸弹的三倍之多。它是从一座500英尺高的塔上投下的，我们的战壕距离爆心约有两英里半。爆炸当量比预期的要大，因此高级军官命令我们撤退到公共汽车上，在那里我们用厨房扫帚互相清扫身上的尘土，然后匆忙坐上位子。

00:53:13.770 --> 00:53:38.370
<v John Conway>The next morning, some of us were bussed back into the area in our fatigues and ordered to walk forward to bear witness. There was still a heavy smell of ozone in the air. The vegetation was shredded, scorched, torn out by the roots, and small animals and birds were scattered, dead, crippled, and blind, lurching about, some still trying to find a place to hide.
<v John Conway>第二天早上，我们中的一些人穿着迷彩服乘车回到该地区，并被命令向前走去见证。空气中仍然弥漫着浓重的臭氧气味。植被被粉碎、烧焦、连根拔起，小动物和鸟类散落得到处都是，死的死，残的残，瞎的瞎，摇摇晃晃地走着，有些还在试图寻找藏身之处。

00:53:38.370 --> 00:54:10.820
<v John Conway>We walked past crumpled vehicles and artillery pieces that had been placed in the open, mannequins staked out, khakis torn apart and melted grotesquely. Caged Cheshire pigs that had been dressed in specially-fitting Army uniforms were dead or mangled, the latter still shrieking their last. Herds of blasted sheep and cows were mangled together, dead or moaning. Staged houses and barns were splintered and scattered.
<v John Conway>我们走过那些被摆在开阔地带的撞得变形的车辆和火炮，还有立着木桩的假人，它们的卡其布衣服被撕裂，怪异地熔化了。那些穿着定制军服的关在笼子里的柴郡猪死伤惨重，后者还在发出最后的尖叫。成群被炸焦的羊和牛混杂在一起，死气沉沉或发出呻吟。搭好的房屋和谷仓四分五裂，散落一地。

00:54:10.820 --> 00:54:40.080
<v John Conway>We never had to write reports, nor did anyone ask us what we saw. Because it turned out, they were looking at us. They wanted to see how a young men reacted to an atomic blast. Apparently, that's all they wanted to do, and I was not selected because I was special. I had no need for qualifications, aside from being a 19-year-old boy.
<v John Conway>我们从来不用写报告，也没有人问我们看到了什么。因为事实证明，他们在观察我们。他们想看看年轻人对原子爆炸会有什么反应。显然，这就是他们想做的一切，而我被选中并不是因为我有什么特别之处。除了是个19岁的男孩之外，我没有任何资格需求。

00:54:40.080 --> 00:55:06.580
<v John Conway>Over the years of America's open-air atomic bomb testing in Nevada, a few thousand Army men participated. It does not matter anymore that only feeble attempts were made by the government to find out how these experiences affected us. Lately, I've begun to realize that I am one of the last people living in America to have actually experienced close-up explosions of atomic bombs.
<v John Conway>在美利坚合众国于内华达州进行露天原子弹试验的多年间，有几千名陆军士兵参与其中。政府只是做出了微不足道的努力去查明这些经历对我们产生了什么影响，这已经不再重要了。最近，我开始意识到，我是美国仅存的几个真正经历过近距离原子弹爆炸的人之一了。

00:55:06.580 --> 00:55:36.340
<v John Conway>We're all dying now, and most of us are already dead. I'm not aware of anyone's health being affected by the blasts, but some years ago, I did check with the Veterans Administration about the possible radiation dosage I received during my participation. I was informed that the radiation film badge that I wore throughout my four months at Desert Rock had been burned up in a government warehouse records fire in St. Louis some decades ago.
<v John Conway>我们现在都在老去，而且我们大多数人已经离世了。我不知道是否有人健康受到了爆炸的影响，但在多年前，确实向退伍军人事务部门咨询过我在参与期间可能受到的辐射剂量。我被告知，我在沙漠岩石营度过的四个月里一直佩戴的辐射胶片徽章，几十年前在圣路易斯的政府仓库档案火灾中被烧毁了。

00:55:36.340 --> 00:56:18.040
<v John Conway>Now, in my late years, when I can conjure that brief, surreal period of my youth, I try in vain to make some sense of it. And in some ways, it has become my responsibility to, at least, recollect and tell how that great flash and blast permanently reached into my very young mind and heart. How those enormous sounds deadened my ears and still ring in them to this day. How the crush of shock waves sometimes buried us alive in our trenches. I feel it my duty to tell the reckless absurdity of it all.
<v John Conway>如今，在我的晚年，当我回想起我那短暂而超现实的青春岁月时，我徒劳地试图去理解它。在某些方面，这已经成了我的责任：至少要回忆并讲述那个巨大的闪光和爆炸是如何永远地触及我极其年轻的心灵；那些巨大的声音是如何震聋我的耳朵，至今仍在耳边回响；冲击波的挤压有时是如何将我们活埋在战壕里。我觉得我有义务去讲述这一切鲁莽的荒谬。

00:56:18.040 --> 00:56:45.590
<v John Conway>In August, our president threatened to unleash fire and fury like the world has never seen. We keep threatening to unleash these bombs, so I suspect that, one day, we will. Most of us have forgotten what we are capable of. I have not.
<v John Conway>八月份，我们的总统威胁要释放前所未见的烈怒与烈火。我们不断威胁要释放这些炸弹，所以我猜，总有一天我们会这么做的。我们大多数人都忘记了我们有能力做出什么。我没有忘。

00:56:45.590 --> 00:57:00.920
<v Ira Glass>John Conley, reading an essay by Paul Zimmer. A version of this essay was first published in "The Georgia Review." Paul Zimmer died in 2019. He was 85. Over his life, he had health problems that he and his family believe were related to his radiation exposure.
<v Ira Glass>约翰·康利（John Conley）朗读保罗·齐默（Paul Zimmer）的一篇散文。该散文的一个版本最初发表在《佐治亚评论》（The Georgia Review）上。保罗·齐默于2019年逝世，享年85岁。在他的一生中，他曾遇到过健康问题，他和家人认为这些问题与他受到的辐射暴露有关。

00:57:00.920 --> 00:57:01.960
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "KIDS" BY MIKKY EKKO]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 米基·埃科的《孩子们》]

00:57:01.960 --> 00:57:22.160
<v Mikky Ekko>(SINGING) Oh, I'm going to let the future in, the future in. I'll take it on, make it on my own. Oh, into the fire, we go again. We go again. We shake it off before we get too old.
<v Mikky Ekko>（歌唱）哦，我要让未来进来，让未来进来。我会承担这一切，靠自己去实现。哦，我们再次投入火海。我们再次出发。在变得太老之前，我们甩掉这一切。

00:57:22.160 --> 00:57:44.600
